 assignment presentations\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Group assignment presentations\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Group assignment presentations\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. identify and analyse major current trends in conducting digital business and understand the key challenges in developing and managing businesses in online contexts\", \"LO2. diagnose and solve business problems and design business solutions that can result in significant business improvement through the exploitation of information and communications technologies\", \"LO3. perform as effective team members in a variety of business contexts and act in a professional business manner as both members and leaders of teams, and in relation to client interactions\", \"LO4. communicate a business plan in a clear and convincing manner both written and verbal\", \"LO5. identify market opportunities and develop strategies to take advantage of them.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Early feedback quiz: This quiz will be based on the content delivered during weeks 1-2 of the semester. Students are required to attempt all questions.\", \"Mid-semester exam: This exam will be based on the content delivered during weeks 3-5 of the semester. Students are required to attempt all questions.\", \"Group project report: The group project is an applied assignment that involves the development of a business plan for a new product or service of your choice. You will outline the value proposition, define the key components of the digital business model, analyse competitive positioning, articulate the digital business capabilities required, and develop an implementation plan. Groups will submit a report that captures these elements.\", \"Group project presentation: Each group will present its business plan during the lectures and tutorials in weeks 12 and 13. All group members must participate in the presentation.\", \"Final exam: This is a closed-book exam that will cover material from all weekly classes and readings. This exam will consist of an extended answer, case study and multiple-choice questions.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS2030/2026-S1C-ND-CC\"]}'),(6,6,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:39.749662','Working in High-Performing Project Teams','{\"level\": \"UG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"INFO3402 or ENGG1850\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"INFS1000 or INFS1020 or INFO1000 or INFO1003 or INFO1903\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"Projects are a common way of managing organisational transformation and change, the development of new products and the implementation of Information Technology (IT) in business. Information Systems (IS) business analysts will work in projects and need an understanding of both project management and the project environment. This unit will introduce you to the end-to-end project management lifecycle as described in the Project Management Body of Knowledge (PMBOK). You will learn how to successfully manage projects from initiation through execution to completion. The focus of this unit will be on the management, execution, and coordination of project activities. To this end, you will learn hands-on project management techniques and gain first-hand experience with a modern online project management platform, including an introduction to agile project management methodologies.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS2040\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS2040/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Working in High-Performing Project Teams\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period\", \"type\": \"Written exam\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"50%\", \"description\": \"Final exam n/a\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 03 Due date: 15 Aug 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 22 Aug 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"600 words\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Project Guide Early feedback\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 26 Oct 2025 at 23:59 Closing date: 02 Nov 2025\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"n/a\", \"weight\": \"40%\", \"description\": \"Group assignment Group assignment around the case study and the team\'s approach\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:01.008834+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction to project management and teams\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"The project management lifecycles + CX -UX -UI: I have to do what exactly? Is this the right thing to even be doing?\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Agile and procedural management and the role of the project manager : So you want to run this thing?\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"The triple constraint and scope management: we are doing what exactly?\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Cost and resource management: how much is this going to be?\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Risk management: do you feel lucky?\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Time and people management: how to plan and how to motivate\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Scheduling teams and resolving disputes: hard and soft PM skills\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Adaptive and agile management: a different way of working\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Adaptive and agile management: At scale DevOps\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Procurement & Stakeholder Management - what resources do I need and how do I communicate with stakeholders? Resolving disputes\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Quality and communications management: getting it right internally and externally\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Unit review. Best way to pass the exam!\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. describe the key phases of the project management life cycle including the tools and methods used in each of those phases\", \"LO2. describe and explain the need for project management in the context of information systems (IS)\", \"LO3. recognise the key attributes of alternative IS project management methodologies including Agile methods\", \"LO4. analyse critically past Information Systems projects, explaining and justifying key themes that contributed to the success or failure of those projects\", \"LO5. set up and manage a project using an online project management platform\", \"LO6. describe and explain contemporary IS trends and issues that impact IS project management\", \"LO7. learn about design thinking and how to manage ideas in a team setting, both hard and soft skills.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Group assignment: Utilising an online project management platform, groups are required to organise their work and develop a report relating to specific information systems (IS) project management issue or trend. Students will be provided a list of contemporary IS project management trends/issues in week 2. Each group must select one of these topics and submit their nominated topic for approval to the unit coordinator by week 4. No more than three groups may select the same issue/trend.\", \"Project Guide: Individual conceptualisation of the nature of projects both formation and execution as a written individual submission.\", \"Written exam. A final assessment that pulls together the concepts and implications of project development and management. This is focused on concepts as discussed in the lectures, not defintions. Importantly this builds on both lecture material and your group work.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS2040/2026-S2C-ND-CC\"]}'),(7,7,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:39.780741','Data Governance and Technology Assurance','{\"level\": \"UG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"INFS3010 or INFS3030\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"INFS1000 or INFO1000 or INFO1003 or INFO1903\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"Data governance is a major imperative for organisations in effectively managing, using, protecting and leveraging their critical data assets. This unit introduces students to key concepts, processes, technologies and stakeholders related to the design and implementation of a data governance program. The unit takes an interdisciplinary and multi-level approach that examines standards, frameworks and methodologies for managing data quality, protecting critical and sensitive information, supporting business analytics and meeting compliance obligations. In examining different stages of the data lifecycle, students also learn about legal, professional and ethical responsibilities, policy implications, required skill sets and accountabilities.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS2050\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS2050/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Data Governance and Technology Assurance\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Week 03 Due date: 13 Mar 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 23 Mar 2026\", \"type\": \"Contribution\", \"length\": \"2 minutes presentation; 2 posts\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Personal Learning Objectives Presentation and responses to presentations by others.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 06 Due date: 02 Apr 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 16 Apr 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"1500 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Individual Assignment One Written task\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 18 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 18 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"2000 words\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Group Assignment Group Report Submission\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 18 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 18 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"-\", \"weight\": \"6%\", \"description\": \"Group Assignment: Presentation Group Presentation\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 22 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 22 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Evaluation\", \"length\": \"-\", \"weight\": \"4%\", \"description\": \"Group Assignment: Individual Evaluation Individual Evaluation\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 29 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 12 Jun 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"1800 words\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Individual Assignment Two Written task\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:01.044346+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction: The Data Governance Imperative\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction: The Data Governance Imperative\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Data governance: definitions, principles and frameworks (Part 1)\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Data governance: definitions, principles and frameworks (Part 1)\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Data governance: definitions, principles and frameworks (Part 2)\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Data governance: definitions, principles and frameworks (Part 2)\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Directed data, surveillance and governance\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Directed data, surveillance and governance\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Volunteered data, analytics and governance\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Volunteered data, analytics and governance\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Self Directed Learning and Consultation\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Self directed learning and consultation\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Automated data, platforms and governance\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Automated data, platforms and governance\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Data ethics and data governance\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Data ethics and data governance\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Data integrity and data governance\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Data integrity and data governance\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Data security and data governance\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Data security and data governance\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Self directed learning and consultation\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Self directed learning and consultation\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Group Presentation and Evaluation\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Group Presentation and Evaluation\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Data Governance Evaluation and Application\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Data Governance Evaluation and Application\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. describe key concepts and principles of data governance and explain their importance in business information systems\", \"LO2. demonstrate knowledge of the relationship of data governance with security, privacy, ethics and regulatory functions\", \"LO3. identify key stakeholders involved in governing data in different and changing institutional contexts and technology platforms\", \"LO4. demonstrate knowledge of key data governance standards and frameworks and practise their application through class activities and assigned tasks\", \"LO5. conduct research to develop and support arguments about key issues, challenges and trends associated with data governance in business\", \"LO6. communicate in oral and written form your knowledge, thoughts and findings through class discussions, group work and individual assignments\", \"LO7. provide constructive feedback to your peers on their written work, and address issues identified by your instructor and peers when reflecting and revising your own written work.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"The assessments support the learning objectives of the unit.\", \"Individual Assignment One: This assignment will require you to integrate information from lectures and tutorials to create a concise written report.\", \"Individual Assignment Two: This assignment will require you to integrate information from lectures and tutorials to create a concise written report.\", \"Group Assignment: This assignment consists of a presentation as well as other written and non-written elements.\", \"Personal Learning Objectives (PLO): In week 3 you will present your PLO for this unit and respond to the PLO of your peers.\", \"Submission links and more information about each assessment are available on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS2050/2026-S1C-ND-CC\"]}'),(8,8,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:39.828748','Enterprise Systems and Integrated Business','{\"level\": \"UG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"INFS1000 or INFO1000 or INFO1003 or INFO1903\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"This unit provides students with an in-depth understanding of the way in which implementation and use of large scale integrated Enterprise Systems change the nature of organisational capabilities, processes, and roles. Students will gain an understanding of the strategic role of Enterprise Systems in providing a platform for improved business operations and designing information infrastructures. In this unit, students will gain considerable hands-on experience with an enterprise-wide system, such as SAP, concentrating on the way in which such systems support integrated business processes. Through a combination of discussion and practical work, students will gain a strong knowledge in both the organisational and technical aspects of Enterprise Systems. Students will also explore the emergence and implications of cloud-based Enterprise Systems and the implementation process. Assumed knowledge for this unit is INFS1000 or equivalent.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS3040\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3040/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Enterprise Systems and Integrated Business\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period\", \"type\": \"Written exam\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"50%\", \"description\": \"Final Exam n/a\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 08 Due date: 25 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 02 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"1000 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Individual assignment n/a\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 11 Due date: 23 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 28 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"2500 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Group project n/a\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:01.858786+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction to Business Processes and Enterprise Systems\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Financial accounting 1\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Financial accounting 2\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Procurement 1\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Procurement 2\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"CATWOE 1 & Enterprise Systems and Information Infrastructures\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"CATWOE 2 & Software Vendor Evaluation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"ES Trends – Cloud Applications\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Managing ES – Risks and Controls\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Managing ES Projects\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Group Assignment Work and Consultations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Group Presentations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Review and Preparation for the Final Exam\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. demonstrate your understanding of the integrated nature of Enterprise Systems and their strategic role in providing a platform for improved business operations\", \"LO2. demonstrate your understanding of the role of Enterprise Systems in information infrastructures and evaluate the organisational effects of increasing integration and standardisation\", \"LO3. demonstrate your understanding of the issues associated with implementing and supporting Enterprise Systems.\", \"LO4. demonstrate your understanding of the change undergone in organisations during the implementation and use of Enterprise Systems\", \"LO5. demonstrate your theoretical understanding of the organisational structures, master data and processes within SAP\", \"LO6. demonstrate your hands-on experience in executing transactions and analysing information using SAP\", \"LO7. work both independently and as a responsible member of a diverse team, collaborate and communicate in a professional manner with people from diverse backgrounds.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Individual assignment: Based upon a business case and your own independent research, you are required to complete hands-on exercises in SAP and design an SAP organisational structure for an implementation project.\", \"Group project: Based upon a business case and the group\'s independent research, students are required to analyse an information infrastructure and identify requirements for an Enterprise Systems solution.\", \"Final exam: This is a closed book exam. Students will need to demonstrate their understanding of all material covered in the unit of study.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3040/2026-S2C-ND-CC\"]}'),(9,9,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:39.859679','Business Intelligence for Managers','{\"level\": \"UG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"INFS1000 or INFO1000 or INFO1003 or INFO1903\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"To gain or maintain their competitive edge, more than ever before, organisations need to rely on high-quality information to support decision making processes at all organisational levels. Business Intelligence (BI) is now being recognised as one of the top business priorities world-wide. While in the past, the term BI was used to describe a very broad range of software applications, the latest thinking in this field emphasises IS support for human intelligence, in the context of business decision making. In this unit, students learn how BI helps information discovery and how to analyse multidimensional data. Students gain hands-on experience in using a commercial BI platform. These practical skills, combined with in-depth analytical skills enable students to assist any organization (regardless of its size and industry domain) to derive more intelligence from its data, improve its performance and ultimately, compete on analytics. Issues are explored from the business rather than the technology perspective. This unit does not require prior programming experience.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS3050\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3050/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Business Intelligence for Managers\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period\", \"type\": \"Written exam\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Final exam BringYourOwnDevice exam on campus\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 05\", \"type\": \"Data analysis\", \"length\": \"n/a\", \"weight\": \"3%\", \"description\": \"Tutorial work 1 Tableau tutorial 1 - multiple due dates\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 06\", \"type\": \"Data analysis\", \"length\": \"n/a\", \"weight\": \"7%\", \"description\": \"Tutorial work 2 Tableau tutorial 2 - multiple due dates\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 08 Due date: 26 Apr 2026 at 12:10\", \"type\": \"Written test\", \"length\": \"1 hour\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Mid-semester exam BringYourOwnDevice exam on campus\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 18 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 28 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Data analysis\", \"length\": \"2500 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Practical Assignment - Data Visualisation Solution and Reflection Analysing the BI process and data visualisation using Tableau\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"-\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Practical Assignment - Presentation Analysing the BI process and data visualisation using Tableau\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:01.887927+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction to unit of study\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Foundations for understanding business intelligence\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Data Warehousing: the business perspective\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Data quality and value of information\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Information discovery and decision making\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Designing business intelligence solutions\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Preparation for the mid semester exam and comprehensive case discussion\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Mid-semester exam\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Multi-dimensional modeling, OLAP and ethics\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Business performance management: the role of business intelligence and data visualisation. AI Support for Decision Making.\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Group project work and consultations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Group presentations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Preparation for the final exam\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. use appropriate conceptual frameworks to analyse the role of different business intelligence applications in supporting day to day business and improving organisational and competitive advantage\", \"LO2. describe the organisational issues related to a business intelligence based management approach and propose solutions to overcome the issues\", \"LO3. design a business intelligence solution to address a business problem using a commercial business intelligence platform\", \"LO4. relate business intelligence to business performance management, business process management and information management in an enterprise\", \"LO5. demonstrate an awareness of the current and emerging trends in business intelligence.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Tutorial work 1 and 2: Students are expected to demonstrate the understanding of the basics of using Tableau and designing a visualisation solution. The tutorials are done during normal class time and must be submitted by the end of the class time.\", \"Mid-semester exam: This exam tests the understanding of the foundational business intelligence concepts.\", \"Practical assignment: Students are required to develop a data visualisation solution to address a business problem using Tableau. A data set will be provided to students. Each group will present their results in the Campus sessions on week 12.\", \"Final exam: The final exam tests the understanding of business intelligence concepts and frameworks in an organisational context. This exam covers all the unit materials. Students will be assessed in a Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) exam taken on campus with supervision. Further information about the delivery of the exams will be provided closer to the test exam.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3050/2026-S1C-ND-CC\"]}'),(10,10,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:39.895709','Business Process Analysis and Design','{\"level\": \"UG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"INFS2020\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"INFS1000 or INFO1000 or INFO1003 or INFO1903\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"This unit provides an in-depth understanding of the role of business processes in effectively and efficiently managing business operations. Students gain essential skills of the entire Business Process Management (BPM) lifecycle, from process identification and design to process monitoring, including process modelling, analysis, redesign, and automation required to achieve high performance and deliver the strategy in a service-oriented environment. Students also gain highly sought after practical skills of using BPM tools to recommend improvements by documenting and simulating current and new processes.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS3120\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3120/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Business Process Analysis and Design\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period\", \"type\": \"Written exam\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"40%\", \"description\": \"Final Exam Written exam\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 06 Due date: 02 Apr 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 09 Apr 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"Up to 1750 Words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Individual Assignment Written submission\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 09 Due date: 27 Apr 2026 at 09:00 Closing date: 29 Apr 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"Up to 500 words (6 PPT slides)\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Group Plan Pitch (5 min) Presentation & Submission\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 29 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 30 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"Up to 2750 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Group Project Group Report Written Submission\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.004565+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction to the Unit and Business Process Management (BPM)\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Process identification\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Process discovery\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Process modelling (1) - BPMN Notation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Process modelling (2) - As-Is Modelling using BPMN and Signavio\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Process analysis (1) (qualitative)\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Process analysis (2) (quantitative): As-Is Simulation using Signavio\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Process improvement (1) - Redesign heuristics\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Group plan presentations - Feedback collection\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Process improvement (2) - To-Be Simulation using Signavio\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Process implementation, monitoring and mining\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Emerging topics in BPM; Group project consultation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Unit Review and Final Exam Practice\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Discuss the role of Business Process Management (BPM) in a business environment and how BPM initiatives contribute to improving the overall business performance\", \"LO2. Apply the BPM principles, concepts, and frameworks to business problems and practice as captured in the BPM lifecycle\", \"LO3. Address the issues and challenges associated with process improvement initiatives in organisations\", \"LO4. Evaluate the impact of process design and redesign decisions on stakeholders and the role of change management in addressing the risks associated with such changes in organisations\", \"LO5. Use BPM tools to model, document, and analyse processes during the BPM lifecycle stages\", \"LO6. Work independently, collaborate, and communicate in a professional manner with people from diverse backgrounds within a team work environment\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Individual assignment: In this assignment students will take the role of the process analyst and engage in process discovery and analysis within a given business case context.\", \"Group Plan assignment: In this assignment students will analyse the market and identify new services to be executed via new and modified business processes. Students will also develop a project plan to guide the project execution. Students will present and make a written submission.\", \"Group Report assignment: In this assignment students will design new processes following the services proposed in the Group Plan assignment. Students will assess the feasibility of the new processes, the resources required, and the challenges encountered during process implementation. Students will use Signavio (BPM tool) to document, simulate and analyse the new business processes. Students will present a summary of the findings and recommendations.\", \"Final exam: A supervised exam that will test students theoretical understanding, critical thinking, and ability to apply and evaluate BPM concepts and principles in practice.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment will be released as appropiate on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3120/2026-S1C-ND-CC\"]}'),(11,11,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:39.929018','Industry and Community Project','{\"level\": \"UG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"BUSS3110 or ACCT3400 or BANK3400 or CLAW3400 or FINC3400 or IBUS3400 or MKTG3400 or QBUS3400 or SIEN3400 or WORK3400 or WORK3401\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"72 credit points\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"INFS1000 and INFS1020 and 6 credit points of INFS 2000-level units of study\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"No\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"This unit provides students with the opportunity to address complex problems identified by industry, community, and government organisations. With mentorship from a major industry partner and an academic lead, students integrate their academic skills and knowledge by working in diverse teams across disciplinary boundaries. Students research, analyse and present solutions to a real-world problem, and build on their interpersonal and transferable skills by engaging with and learning from experts in the field. See the ICPU website for further information here: https://www.sydney.edu.au/students/industry-and-community-projects.html.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS3400\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3400/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Industry and Community Project\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Ongoing\", \"type\": \"Contribution\", \"length\": \"NA\", \"weight\": \"0%\", \"description\": \"Participation Compulsory 90% class participation\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 03 Due date: 23 Aug 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 02 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"500 words\", \"weight\": \"0%\", \"description\": \"Individual Statement Instalment 1 Written task\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 05 Due date: 06 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 16 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"2000 words\", \"weight\": \"20%\", \"description\": \"Group Plan Research plan\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 07 Due date: 20 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 30 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"500 words\", \"weight\": \"20%\", \"description\": \"Individual Statement Instalment 2 Written task\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 26 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 05 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"≤10 minutes\", \"weight\": \"20%\", \"description\": \"Group Presentation Oral presentation inclusive of 300-word or equivalent Executive summary\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 06 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 16 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"4000 words\", \"weight\": \"40%\", \"description\": \"Group Project Report Report\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 08 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 18 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Evaluation\", \"length\": \"NA\", \"weight\": \"0%\", \"description\": \"Group Work Survey Questionnaire\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:01.934822+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Weekly\", \"topic\": \"See Canvas site for detailed project schedule\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Weekly\", \"topic\": \"See Canvas site for detailed project schedule\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Demonstrate critical awareness of, and apply, disciplinary knowledge, skills and personal attributes to address complex real-world problems\", \"LO2. Assess and integrate diverse stakeholder perspectives to generate innovative insights into complex problems\", \"LO3. Purposefully select and apply complex problem-solving approaches and methods\", \"LO4. Communicate novel ideas effectively and persuasively in professional settings using relevant media\", \"LO5. Demonstrate professionalism (proficiency, adaptivity, and proactivity) in collaborative project work\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Individual Statements aims to analyse and reflect on students’ own and other members’ ways of contributing to their work on this project.\", \"Group Plan aims to articulate the issues the group will address and develop a project plan.\", \"Group Presentation including Executive Summary aims to present students’ work and findings to the project partner.\", \"Group Project Report aims to conduct in depth analysis/es and research, discuss the results, and provide recommendations or solutions to the issues outlined in the group plan.\", \"Group Work Survey aims to evaluate student’s contributions relative to the contributions of other team members.\", \"Participation aims to ensure active participation by all team members for the project work.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\", \"Individual contributions to group assessments may adjust the final grade that a student receives, through what is known as a Relative Performance Factor. Please see Canvas Assignment page for more details.\", \"Using AI in ICPU\", \"The Use of Generative AI across multiple sectors of work is increasing exponentially. While use of these tools brings new opportunities such as increased productivity, there are also many potential limitations such as inhibiting the development of analytical and creative thinking skills. Importantly, in ICPU where 80% of assessments are group-based, your use of AI tools must also be negotiated collegially and adhere to the Student Charter 2020, including upholding honesty, ethics, professionalism, and academic integrity (please ensure you are familiar with the updated Academic Integrity 2022 policy).\", \"AI tools permitted\", \"You are permitted to use automated writing tools and generative artificial intelligence (AI) to facilitate your learning and completion of this assignment in individual and group project work. Please adhere to the following guidelines:\", \"Do not enter confidential, personal, copyrighted or otherwise sensitive information into any AI tool, including but not limited to materials shared by industry and/or community partners (noting also the confidentiality clauses that apply in the Student Deed Poll)\", \"If you use these tools, you must be aware of their limitations, biases, and propensity for fabrication\", \"Do not rely on the accuracy of outputs. You remain responsible for your work. This means you must independently verify and edit AI-generated content to ensure the integrity, accuracy, and suitability of the output\", \"While there are many AI tools available, both free and paid, we strongly recommend the use of tools endorsed by the University such as Microsoft Copilot, Cogniti and Adobe Firefly. For a list of AI tools and their uses, see the Different Generative AI Tools page on the AI in Education Canvas site.\", \"Where AI is used, you are advised to keep copies of the prompts you provided and AI outputs as evidence of your research and writing process. This allows you to produce an accurate acknowledgement of AI use when you submit your work, see guidance below. This can be requested by the unit coordinator if there is any uncertainty about the originality of your work.\", \"Acknowledging the use of AI\", \"It is not appropriate to copy-paste AI-generated responses directly as part of your own writing since the work you submit should reflect the understanding you have acquired, your critical engagement with the material and your process of continuous revision and improvement.\", \"Where AI is used, you are advised to keep copies of the prompts you provided and AI outputs as evidence of your research and writing process. This allows you to produce an accurate acknowledgement of AI use when you submit your work, see wording below. This can be requested by the unit coordinator if there is any uncertainty about the originality of your work.\", \"You must acknowledge any uses of AI by inserting a note at the end of your submission where you need to describe the tool(s) that you used, what prompt(s) you provided, how the output of the artificial intelligence was used or adapted by you, and how you verified sources or information if applicable. This additional description does not add to your word count. An example of such an acknowledgement that you may provide might look like:\", \"I acknowledge the use of ChatGPT (https://chat.openai.com/) to refine the academic language of my own work. On date I submitted my entire essay (link to original document here) with the prompt to <“Improve the academic tone and accuracy of language, including grammatical structures, punctuation and vocabulary”>. The output (link here) was then modified further to better represent my own tone and style of writing. One of the limitations I noted was insert limitation and I revised my assessment to address this limitation by insert response.\", \"Please note, your project supervisor may require you to provide a written statement if you would like to use AI heavily in your work, (i.e. for multiple approved uses above or heavy use within any individual use categories), a different approach may be needed including clearly stating the percentage of work attributed to AI for each assessment. If you are unsure or have any concerns, please discuss these with your Project Supervisor in advance. In all cases, individual group members must be transparent with their group members about intended use of generative AI in groupwork.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3400/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3400/2026-S1CIFE-BM-CC\", \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3400/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3400/2026-S2CIJL-BM-CC\"]}'),(12,12,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:39.970712','Business Information Systems in Practice','{\"level\": \"UG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"Students commencing from 2018: completion of at least 112 credit points including (INFS1000 or INFO1000 or INFO1003 or INFO1903) and INFS1020. Pre-2018 continuing students: completion of at least 112 credit points including (INFS1000 or INFO1000 or INFO1003 or INFO1903) and INFS2001 and INFS2020\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"All other requirements for the major or program associated with this capstone must be completed prior to or concurrently with (if enrolment rules permit) this unit of study. Capstones must be completed at the University of Sydney Business School only\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"No\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"This capstone unit bridges the gap between theory and practice by integrating knowledge and consolidating key skills developed across the Business Information Systems major. It aims to provide students with practical experience in identifying, analysing, and solving contemporary business problems. Much of this unit is dedicated to a problem-solving, experiential approach to learning. Students ensure their career-readiness by demonstrating their ability to apply concepts, theories, frameworks, methodologies, and skills to authentic problems and challenges faced in the field of information systems.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS3600\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3600/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Business Information Systems in Practice\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Week 03 Due date: 19 Aug 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 29 Aug 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"600 words\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Generative AI Strategy Plan and critically reflect on using GenAI and how it affects learning.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 06 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 20 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"In-person written or creative task\", \"length\": \"1500 words + appendix\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Individual progress report and viva Students submit an individual role-based progress report, assessed through a supervised in-class viva where verified students explain and defend their work. Consultation schedules are on Canvas; the submission deadline is Week 13.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 06 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 20 Dec 2026\", \"type\": \"Q&A following presentation, submission or placement\", \"length\": \"15 minutes (10mins presentation + Q&A)\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Group project presentation Students deliver a prepared presentation, followed by a supervised in-class Q&A. During the Q&A, verified students respond to teaching staff questions under supervision and without unapproved external assistance.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 09 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 16 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"1500 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Reflective Journal Individual, continuous, reflective learning journal, stimulated by prompts.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 06 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 16 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"6000 words\", \"weight\": \"35%\", \"description\": \"Group assignment Written report\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.056806+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Unit Introduction\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Case Study Research Methods\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO4 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Business Problem Identification\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Design Science Research Methods\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Solution Objective\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Prototyping and Storytelling\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Outsourcing and Automation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"IT Project Management\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Usability Engineering\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Design Ethics\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Scientific Writing\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Unit Review and Debriefing\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Project presentations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO4\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. apply and relate information systems theories and concepts in the context of an actual business problem with societal relevance\", \"LO2. analyse open-ended business scenarios and evaluate the implications of information systems decisions\", \"LO3. apply techniques for organisational and business process analyses to resolve an actual business problem\", \"LO4. communicate in a professional and confident manner, both orally and in writing, with actual business professionals\", \"LO5. apply team management concepts and theories to effectively work in, and manage, cross-functional teams\", \"LO6. analyse and reflect critically on the ethical and social implications of information systems.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Individual assignment: This assignment is in the form of an individual progress report that corresponds to a specific section of the group project. The content of this report will vary depending on the student\'s designated role within the project team. The report comprises 1500 (± 10%) words and associated discussions during the group consultation sessions.\", \"Group assignment: This assessment item requires students to work within their project groups to analyse and provide recommendations for resolving a business problem of an actual client. Each project group will be expected to look for and acquire their own client, and design a technology-enabled solution that will improve a substantive business process of the client. The deliverables of the project include a 15 minute presentation and a project report of 6000 (± 10%) words.\", \"Reflective journal: Individual, continuous, reflective journal about the student’s authentic learning experience gleaned from the group project and the in-class activities. The student will draft the reflective journal continuously in Canvas and the teaching team will ask them 4-5 ‘provocative’ questions to prompt their critical reflection throughout the semester. The final deliverable is a written reflective journal of 1500 words (± 10%).\", \"Generative AI Strategy: By developing a clear strategy for using GenAI tools in assignment work, students will enhance their ability to leverage GenAI for tasks such as identifying literature, structuring ideas, refining text, and fact-checking while ensuring ethical and effective practices. The goal is not just to ensure academic integrity but also to reflect on how GenAI tools shape analytical and critical thinking skills. The deliverable is a 600 word (± 10%) document.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3600/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3600/2026-S2C-ND-CC\"]}'),(13,13,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.006495','Foundation in Business Information Systems','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"INFS6001\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"None\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"This unit introduces students to the foundations of business information systems and the emerging strategic role of information and communication technologies (ICT) in transforming organisations. Students develop an understanding of how ICT come to matter to organisations as they disrupt, change and transform organisations and organisational processes. The unit covers systems issues, information literacy and use, and how various digital technologies work together to create an infrastructure for digital business and how technologies (such as social media) are disrupting \'business as usual\'. The role of information systems in capturing and distributing organisational knowledge and in enhancing decision-making is explored. Students gain an understanding of socio-technical networks, how information systems function, and how processes in organisations can be leveraged to create digital innovation and business transformation.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS5002\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS5002/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Foundation in Business Information Systems\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period Due date: 16 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 25 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"2,100 words\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"End of Semester Reflective Essay An end of semester reflection on the unit learning. For details see Canvas.\"}, {\"due\": \"Ongoing\", \"type\": \"Contribution\", \"length\": \"-\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Participation Individual, see canvas for details\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 10 Due date: 12 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 21 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Evaluation\", \"length\": \"-\", \"weight\": \"20%\", \"description\": \"Reflection on Group Work Provide and respond to feedback on the performance of team members.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 04 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 13 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"Page limit of 50 pages (exc appendices)\", \"weight\": \"35%\", \"description\": \"Group Project Design proposal for system, with weekly formative submissions, see Canvas.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 06 Nov 2026 at 07:00 Closing date: 06 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"3-minutes\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Group project Presentation Each team should offer a 3-minute proposal presentation followed by a 5-minute Q&A. see Canvas.\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.046656+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction to Foundation in Digital Business; BIS as Technology\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"BIS as Disruption; BIS as Transformation; BIS as Project\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"BIS as Tool; BIS as Information; Workshop - Information Searching\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Workshop - Design Thinking\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"BIS as System; BIS as System Dynamic\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"BIS as System Thinking; BIS as Designed\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"BIS and IT; BIS as Data; Workshop - Data Flow Diagrams\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Self-directed study and consultation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"BIS as Workflow; Workshop - Introduction to BPMN; BIS as tool for efficiency\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"BIS as Decision; BIS as Insight\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"BIS and Ethics; BIS as Developed\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"BIS and Sustainability; Summary and Farewell\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Group Project pitch/presentation to clients.\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Interpret, analyze, model and visualize representations of business processes, organisational structures and data.\", \"LO2. Appraise and critically assess the use of digital technology in business, society and individual workplace contexts.\", \"LO3. Effectively communicate and collaborate with different personalities, individually and in group settings, incomplex digital work environments to achieve positive and sustainable change outcomes.\", \"LO4. Evaluate the operations of a business and suggest and design solutions and improvements enabled by digital technology.\", \"LO5. Identify current trends in business information systems and ability to assess their implications for managers, organisations, employees and other stakeholders.\", \"LO6. Appraise and Examine the problem space within which technology is used with the aim to argue for the design of a specific solution\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"For details on assessments please see the assessment section and details provided on the Unit’s Canvas site.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS5002/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS5002/2026-S2C-ND-CC\"]}'),(14,14,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.035581','Strategic Information Systems Sourcing','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"Basic business information systems knowledge\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"Strategic Information Systems Sourcing has at its core the cost-effective sourcing and delivery of IS solutions with the aim of supporting and enabling business strategy. It covers the procurement of in-house systems, outsourcing strategies and third-party IS sourcing (for example from \'the cloud\'). The focus of this unit is on managerial decisions in relation to the design, implementation and delivery of an IS sourcing strategy, and the governance of IS sourcing solutions. Students develop knowledge of the concepts, tools and methodologies used in the negotiation and development of an IS sourcing strategy.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS6002\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6002/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Strategic Information Systems Sourcing\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period Due date: 16 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 26 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"3000 words\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Individual assignment II Reflective essay on in-class activities\"}, {\"due\": \"Multiple weeks\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"5 mins\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Case Study Presentation on a chosen case article\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 07 Due date: 15 Sep 2026 at 16:00\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Case study on IT Sourcing Decision Worksheet Presentation Complete in class\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 08 Due date: 25 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 05 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"2000 words\", \"weight\": \"20%\", \"description\": \"Individual assignment I Develop an information systems sourcing plan\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 02 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 12 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"3000 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Group Report IS pitching plan as a vendor\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 02 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 06 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Creative work\", \"length\": \"2 mins 30 seconds\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Video creation Marketing video for a chosen information system\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.130537+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction to IS sourcing\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Strategic Management and Information Systems\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"IS Outsourcing Foundations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Business Process Analysis in IS Sourcing: BAM and CATWOE\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Going to Market\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Evaluating Sourcing Options and Vendors\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Integrated Discussion of Sourcing Decision Making\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Vendors\' perspectives\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Vendors\' perspectives\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"FinOps: Financial Operations for Cloud Cost Optimization\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Sourcing Risks and Control Strategies\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Managing Transition and Post-Sourcing Operations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Revision\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Evaluate opportunities for outsourcing IT services within a range of business settings.\", \"LO2. Demonstrate your understanding of the range of available outsourced IT services by selecting suitable services for a set of provided business scenarios.\", \"LO3. Demonstrate understanding of the theories related to outsourcing by applying them to evaluate various outsourcing scenarios.\", \"LO4. Evaluate strategic information systems sourcing decisions in a business setting.\", \"LO5. Identify risks associated with outsourcing and describe the risk mitigation strategies that are commonly applied and suggest recommendations on suitable controls.\", \"LO6. Demonstrate understanding of the sourcing process from a vendor perspective.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Individual Assessments\", \"Individual Assignment I: Develop an information systems sourcing plan\", \"Individual Assignment II: An reflective essay on in-class activities; consist of multiple components.\", \"Group Assessments:\", \"Case Study Discussion: Every group presents one case study in class. The order is to be determined in class.\", \"In-class Assessment: Work as a group to solve a sourcing decision making case study in class. Including worksheet and presentation.\", \"Group Project Report: Develop an IS pitching proposal as a vendor\", \"Group Project Video: Marketing/Pitching video for a chosen information systems.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6002/2026-S2C-ND-CC\"]}'),(15,15,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.071850','Digital Business Transformation','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"INFS5002 or COMP5206\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"Understanding the major functions of a business and how those business functions interact internally and externally so the company can be competitive in a changing market. How digital technologies can be used and managed in a business. How to critically analyse a business and determine its options for digital transformation. Desirable Assumed Knowledge: Experience as a member of a project team\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal evening] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"The disruptive forces of digital technologies require businesses to be in a constant state of change to stay competitive in turbulent markets. However, as companies need to maintain their current revenue streams, they need to progress through a series of digital transformation projects. In this unit students learn how to analyse an organisation within a local and global context and develop knowledge of techniques required for managing digital business transformation initiatives. Topics covered include: the drivers of digital business transformation, managing digital transformation as a process, understand the power of disruptive technologies, technological impacts on new forms of organizational arrangements.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS6004\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6004/2026-S1C-NE-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Digital Business Transformation\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period\", \"type\": \"Written exam\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"40%\", \"description\": \"Final Exam Written Exam\"}, {\"due\": \"Multiple weeks Due date: 20 Mar 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 27 Mar 2026\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"90-second executive pitch with Q&A\", \"weight\": \"15%\", \"description\": \"Assignment 1 Individual in-class presentation in week 5/6. This is a 90-second pitch accompanied by a one-page poster.\"}, {\"due\": \"Multiple weeks\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"-\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Assignment 2c Group presentation (In-class presentation Week 12/13)\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 07 Due date: 17 Apr 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 01 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"1000 words\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Assignment 2a Group report outline\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 18 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 25 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"3000 words\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Assignment 2b Business report and presentation slides\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.163019+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction and Overview\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Understanding Disruption\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Understanding Disruption\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"The Drivers and Enablers of Digital Transformation\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"The Drivers and Enablers of Digital Transformation\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Manage Change and Transformation-1\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Manage Change and Transformation-1\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Manage Change and Transformation-2\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Manage Change and Transformation-2\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Overcome Challenges of Digital Transformation\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Overcome Challenges of Digital Transformation\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Digital Transformation of Consumption\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Digital Transformation of Consumption\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Digital Transformation of Communication\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Digital Transformation of Communication\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Digital Transformation of Traditional Industries\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Digital Transformation of Traditional Industries\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Group assignment consultation and self-directed learning\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO6 LO7 LO8\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Group assignment consultation and self-directed learning\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO6 LO7 LO8\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Review and Synthesis\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO6 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Group Presentations\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6 LO7 LO8\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Group Presentations\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6 LO7 LO8\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Identify and describe the drivers and enablers of digital transformation in business settings.\", \"LO2. Critically analyse business problems that necessitate digital transformation and change in an organisation.\", \"LO3. Critically and creatively analyse and propose business innovations to address identified business problems.\", \"LO4. Critically analyse the challenges for businesses in the face of innovation and technological advancements.\", \"LO5. Critically and creatively analyse various strategies to overcome challenges in digital transformation.\", \"LO6. Critically synthesise and apply methods, models and frameworks with business analysis, communication and research skills in digital transformation projects.\", \"LO7. Collaborate and communicate in a professional manner with people from diverse backgrounds.\", \"LO8. Contribute effectively as a responsible member of a diverse team for group assessments.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"• Assignment 1 (Executive Pitch and Q&A): Students will select a company that has undergone digital transformation and analyse recent news articles about the company. They will synthesise the key insights and evaluate the likely transformative impact of these developments, and communicate their analysis through a one-page poster and a 90-second executive pitch. Students will be encouraged to focus on an industry and company aligned with their future career interests. • Assignment 2a (Group Report Outline): Students will work in groups as a team of consultants to submit a structured outline for their Digital Transformation Management Plan for a selected company. The outline will present the company background and context, diagnose the need for digital transformation, and propose and briefly evaluate potential digital solutions. This submission is designed to support formative feedback from the teaching team, which groups should use to strengthen their final report and presentation.\", \"• Assignment 2b (Group Report & Presentation Slides): Students will work in groups to develop a professional business report that proposes a digital transformation solution for a selected organisation. Building on feedback from the proposal outline, the report should describe the solution in sufficient detail to show how it addresses the organisation’s challenges, and apply current change management frameworks to analyse the organisation and develop a transformative change management plan aligned with the solution and project requirements.\", \"• Assignment 2c (In-class presentation): All students in each team to design, develop, and present the critical details from Assignment 2b (group report).\", \"• Final Exam: This exam will focus on the fundamental concepts and applied and experiential knowledge acquired across the semester. Note: This assessment will take the form of a supervised exam. It is a closed- book exam; no materials are allowed.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6004/2026-S1C-NE-CC\"]}'),(16,16,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.115606','Digital Information Infrastructures','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"INFS5002 or COMP5206\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"None\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"The unit provides an overall introduction to the different components of digital information infrastructures in organisational contexts. This includes organisational enterprise systems architecture, legacy systems, enterprise systems, cloud infrastructure and enabling technologies such as blockchain, internet of things, or web services. Strategic managerial issues that arise from the implementation, use, and security management of information infrastructures and enterprise systems are also covered. The unit utilises a combination of practical sessions with Enterprise Systems, such as SAP, and case study analyses to build a deep understanding of relevant topics.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS6012\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6012/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Digital Information Infrastructures\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period\", \"type\": \"Written exam\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"50%\", \"description\": \"Final Exam Closed book exam\"}, {\"due\": \"Multiple weeks\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"-\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Case Presentation Presentation on Case Study Articles\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 08 Due date: 24 Apr 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 01 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Practical skill\", \"length\": \"-\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Individual Assignment Written report with specified questions\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 22 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 01 Jun 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"-\", \"weight\": \"17%\", \"description\": \"Group project report Report\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 22 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 01 Jun 2026\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"-\", \"weight\": \"3%\", \"description\": \"Group project presentation Report\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.306243+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Business Process and Enterprise Systems\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Enterprise Systems and Accounting Process I\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Enterprise Systems and Procurement Process I\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Enterprise Systems and Procurement Process II\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Enterprise Systems and Lead to Cash Process\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Integrated Discussion on Enterprise Systems\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Guest Lecture (TBC)\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Cloud Computing and Enterprise Systems\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Blockchain and IoT\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Managing Digital Information Infrastructure I\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Managing Digital Information Infrastructure II\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Course Revision\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO6\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. explore the strategic managerial issues that arise from the implementation and use of enterprise systems as a means of integrating data and standardising processes\", \"LO2. use an example enterprise system to gain hands-on competency in supporting key business processes\", \"LO3. provide an overview of an enterprise system implementation solution which addresses key processes, data and organisational structures, and explain the reasons behind the solution\", \"LO4. evaluate the place of enterprise systems in broader digital information infrastructure.\", \"LO5. evaluate in a critical manner the technical trends which may impact on future enterprise systems use\", \"LO6. present ideas and arguments effectively, deliver a comprehensive and integrated enterprise information infrastructure that reflects a collective effort from a professional project team\", \"LO7. apply the principles of IT resources management to effectively solve real-world problems.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Individual assignment: The individual assignment consists of multiple components that may include the completion of tasks with SAP and a written report.\", \"Group project: You must demonstrate an information infrastructure which explains the role of enterprise systems within that infrastructure, discusses organisational and change management issues, and examines the effect of new technological trends.\", \"Case presentation: Each group is scheduled for a 10-minute case presentation during different weeks. The goal of these presentations is to help the class better understand the case background.\", \"Final exam: This is a closed-book exam and will assess your ability to demonstrate your understanding of all material covered in the unit of study.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6012/2026-S1C-ND-CC\"]}'),(17,17,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.148956','Business Process Management','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"INFS5002 or COMP5206\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"None\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal evening] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"This unit highlights the key concepts of business process management (BPM) and their importance to organisations in supporting the business operations and providing opportunities for competitive advantage and innovation. It provides a detailed understanding of the concepts, strategies, tools and technologies required throughout the BPM lifecycle. This unit takes both the strategic and change management perspectives in designing process architecture and governance, as well as analysing, designing, and measuring performance of processes across value chains and aligned to industry standards. The unit also develops practical skills in modelling and simulating processes using business process management tools.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS6015\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6015/2026-S2C-NE-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Business Process Management\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period\", \"type\": \"Written exam\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"40%\", \"description\": \"Final exam Written exam\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 06 Due date: 11 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 21 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"Up to 1750 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Individual assignment Written submission\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 09 Due date: 06 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 16 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Creative work\", \"length\": \"500 words (6 PPT slides)\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Business Proposal Business idea and execution plan proposal\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 08 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 09 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"Up to 2750 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Business Report Group Report Submission\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.363158+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Overview of Business Process Management (BPM)\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Process identification\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Process governance and discovery\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO4 LO5 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Process modelling 1: BPMN Notation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Process modelling 2: Modelling As-Is processes using Signavio\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Process analysis 1 (qualitative)\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Process analysis 2 (quantitative): Simulating As-Is processes using Signavio\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Process redesign 1: Redesign heuristics\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Public Holiday (Mon) - No class; Business Proposal due for Feedback collection\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Process redesign 2: Simulating To-Be processes using Signavio\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Process implementation, monitoring, and mining\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"BPM emerging topics; Group project consultation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Unit Review and Final Exam Preparation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Discuss the holistic role of Business Process Management (BPM) in a business environment to improve performance and align to overall strategy.\", \"LO2. Apply BPM methodologies, concepts, and principles to business problems and practice following the BPM lifecycle.\", \"LO3. Address the issues and challenges associated with process change initiatives in organisations.\", \"LO4. Evaluate the impact of process change projects on stakeholders and the role of change management in mitigating risks in such projects.\", \"LO5. Use BPM tools to model, document, and analyse processes during the BPM lifecycle.\", \"LO6. Work independently in a professional and ethical manner\", \"LO7. Work as a responsible member of a diverse team, collaborate and communicate in a professional manner with people from diverse backgrounds.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Individual assignment: In this assignment students will take the role of the process analyst and engage in process discovery and analysis within a given business case context.\", \"Business Proposal assignment: In this asignment students will analyse the market and identify new services for a business case seeking to expand. Students will propose a new service and a plan to guide the execution of the project.\", \"Business Report assignment: In this assignment students will desing new processes following the service proposed in their Business Proposal pitch. Students will assess the feasibility of new processes, the resources required, and the challenges encountered during process implementation. Students will use Signavio (BPM tool) to document, simulate and analyse the new business processes.\", \"Final exam: A supervised exam that will test students’ theoretical understanding, critical thinking, and ability to apply and evaluate BPM concepts and principles in practice.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6015/2026-S2C-NE-CC\"]}'),(18,18,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.183839','Digital Business Models','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"INFS5002 or COMP5206\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"Understanding the major functions of a business and how those business functions interact internally and externally so the company can be competitive in the market is essential in order to critically analyse how and where a business can be innovative. Some knowledge of how technology can be applied in a business is also essential. Experience as a member of a project team is desirable\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"This unit develops knowledge and skills in innovative, digital business models and strategies from a management perspective. The unit facilitates a better understanding and application of the concepts, strategies, tools and technologies necessary for undertaking digital innovation of business models. From basic knowledge of digital business models and business processes, this unit increases awareness and understanding of stakeholders, their capabilities and their limitations in the strategic convergence of technology and business. It provides insights into the digital technologies required to support business in the 21st Century and supports development of student capabilities to analyse, develop and evaluate innovative digital business strategies and models.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS6016\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6016/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Digital Business Models\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Multiple weeks\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"600 words each\", \"weight\": \"24%\", \"description\": \"Individual reflective reports Individual reflective reports\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 09 Due date: 01 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 15 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"2500 words\", \"weight\": \"26%\", \"description\": \"Individual project Written report\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 22 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 29 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"5000 words\", \"weight\": \"40%\", \"description\": \"Group project report Written report\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 26 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 29 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"15 minutes\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Group project presentation Group project presentation\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.369553+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction and overview\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Perspectives on digital innovation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Digital platform- 1\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Digital platform- 2\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Business model in the digital era-1\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Business model in the digital era-2\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Group project activity\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Group project activity\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Self-directed learning & Group assignment consultation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Digital business model- industry-specific development\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Digital business model- industry-specific development\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Review and synthesis\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Group Presentation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Demonstrate knowledge and understanding of characteristics of digital business\", \"LO2. Effectively analyze and evaluate the strategy and business model of digital platforms\", \"LO3. Demonstrate capabilities for critical analysis and creative design of practical digital business innovations\", \"LO4. Critically assess the value and opportunities of emerging technologies for business innovations in different industries\", \"LO5. Collaborate and communicate in a professional manner with people from diverse backgrounds\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Individual report: Students will select, analyse, and submit a written report of a recent digital platform business relevant to the learning objectives for this unit.\", \"Group project report: The group project report is an applied assignment that requires the team\'s original development of an innovative digital business plan.\", \"Group project presentation: All students in each team will design, develop, and present the critical details from the group assignment report.\", \"Individual reflective reports: Students will write several reflective reports on the unit learning.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6016/2026-S1C-ND-CC\"]}'),(19,19,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.219614','Managing with Information and Data','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"INFS5002 or COMP5206 or QBUS5001\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"Understanding the major functions of a business and how those business functions interact Semester 1 internally and externally so the company can be competitive in a changing market. How information systems can be used and managed in a business. How to critically analyse a business and determine its options for transformation. Desirable Experience as a member of a project team\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"Business analytics and the ability to interpret and respond to related outputs, is a major source of competitive advantage in the information age and is therefore a leading business priority globally. In recent times, this field has evolved from a technology topic to a management priority, creating an unprecedented demand for new competencies in managing with data. Taking a business rather than technology perspective, this unit covers the enterprise ecosystem in the context of strategic and operational analytics and decision making. Topics include innovation through advanced analytics, data driven performance management, strategic business improvement and management of complex BI projects. The unit offers hands-on experience in using a commercial platform, combined with in-depth analytical skills, and enables students completing the unit to help any organization to derive more value from data and information and compete on analytics.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS6018\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6018/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Managing with Information and Data\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Multiple weeks Due date: 31 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 10 Jun 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"2500 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Reflective Essay Written essay on core learnings\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 07 Due date: 19 Apr 2026 at 15:10\", \"type\": \"Written test\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"35%\", \"description\": \"Mid-semester test Mid-semester test will assess core learnings from weeks 1-5\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 18 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 28 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Data analysis\", \"length\": \"4000 words\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Project report Written report\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"Poster + 5 min Presentation + 15 min Q&A\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Group project presentation Group presentation of written report and related analysis\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.368439+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Foundational and historical concepts\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Decision making for managers\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Data quality\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Strategies & business models for valuecreation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Strategic alignment and IT governance\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Exam preparation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Mid-semester Test\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Analytics, people and culture\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Ethics for business intelligence\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Managing the change process and stakeholders\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Guest Lecture\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Managing emerging technologies: Blockchain and AI\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Managing the cloud\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Apply the fundamental concepts and practice of managing through data challenges in differentiated organisational settings.\", \"LO2. Identify and analyse issues, challenges and solutions in managing through data/business analytics systems from the business rather thantechnical perspective.\", \"LO3. Critically examine the intra and inter organisational and societal issues involved in implementing various types of analytical systems.\", \"LO4. Design and implement a small-scale data analysis project in an organisational setting.\", \"LO5. Evaluate different analytical outputs and their ethical impacts.\", \"LO6. Apply advanced principles and techniques related to strategy, organizational change and information governance to help organisations effectively maximise value from their data, information assets and technology investments.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Mid-semester test to be held in Week 7 on core concepts delivered in weeks 1-5.\", \"Group project and report: In groups, students will use Tableau to explore a real-world data set and consider the implications for distinct personas. Students will prepare a report outlining this analysis. Students will be orally examined in groups on the contents of this report.\", \"Reflective essay: Students will utilise a reflective framework to critically evaluate managerial practices\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6018/2026-S1C-ND-CC\"]}'),(20,20,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.251101','Data Visualisation for Managers','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"None\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"Data visualisation, storytelling, and scenario development are prominent analytical practices that are increasingly used by professionals seeking to use data for decision making. This unit aims to equip students with necessary knowledge and data visualisation skills, acquired through real-world projects and applications inspired by leading industry practices. Students develop a holistic view of data visualisation and acquire knowledge of related tools to deal with organisational and societal challenges. This unit focuses on business/organisational decision makers and their use of data visualisation. As such this unit does not require any prior IT, computer science or data science experience.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS6023\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6023/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Data Visualisation for Managers\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Multiple weeks\", \"type\": \"Practical skill\", \"length\": \"5 hours\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Skills-based assessment Self-paced workshop\"}, {\"due\": \"Multiple weeks\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"10 minutes\", \"weight\": \"20%\", \"description\": \"Case study discussion Presentation\"}, {\"due\": \"Multiple weeks\", \"type\": \"Conversation\", \"length\": \"10 minutes\", \"weight\": \"20%\", \"description\": \"In-class assessment Case study discussion\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 06 Due date: 11 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 21 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"1500 words\", \"weight\": \"15%\", \"description\": \"Individual assignment Written report\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 11 Due date: 23 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 02 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"3000 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Group project report Written report\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 11 Due date: 23 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 02 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"10 minutes\", \"weight\": \"15%\", \"description\": \"Group project presentation Presentation\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.389482+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction to Data Visualisation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Conceptual Foundations of Data Visualisation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Designing Effective Visualisations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Knowledge Discovery via Data Analytics\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Data Quality\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Comprehensive Case Discussion\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Ethics of Data Visualisation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Infographics\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Visual Storytelling\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Visualisation Principles and DV Lifecycle\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Guided Project Work\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Group Presentations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Group Presentations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Apply fundamental concepts of data visualisation to analyse and present data-related challenges in organisational and societal settings.\", \"LO2. Identify and analyse multi-disciplinary issues and ethical challenges associated with the use of data visualisation techniques.\", \"LO3. Explain principles and best practices of data visualisation as it applies to current and emerging industry trends.\", \"LO4. Design and develop data visualisations based on real world datasets.\", \"LO5. Recommend and evaluate data visualisation solutions and present outcomes orally and verbally.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Case Study Discussion: The purpose of this assignment is for students to apply data visualisation concepts to real-world business cases and datasets. Case studies will be assigned on a weekly basis and discussed in class.\", \"In-class Assessment: The purpose of this assignment is for students to evaluate and discuss data visualsation solutions as they are being applied to real-world business cases and datasets. Case studies will be assigned on a weekly basis and discussed in class.\", \"Individual Assignment: The purpose of this assignment is for students to use data visualisation techniques to address a business problem within a particular business case context.\", \"Group Project Report: The purpose of this assignment is to enable students to explain principles and best practices of data visualisation as it applies to a business or societal context, conduct research, stimulate broader thinking through collaborative teamwork and present their findings in written format.\", \"Group Project Presentation: The purpose of this assignment is for students to evaluate and recommend data visualisation solutions in a manner relevant to decision makers. The assignment will assess students on their ability to utilize essential business communication skills and correspond with an audience in oral and interactive forms.\", \"Skills-based Assessment: The purpose of this assignment is for students to develop the skills to design and develop data visualisation solutions. This are self-paced skill development exercises that have to be completed throughout the semester.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6023/2026-S2C-ND-CC\"]}'),(21,21,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.298795','Managing Data at Scale','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"None\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal evening] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"As the amount of data generated continues to grow dramatically, managing data at scale presents a set of challenges for which organizations have to be uniquely prepared. This unit provides students with what they need to know about generating, collecting, processing, storing, managing, analysing and interpreting data. The unit provides an understanding of the importance of data strategies, data governance and data quality, challenges associated with data protection, privacy and security, data management practices, and modern platforms and architecture to streamline data operations within an organisation. Students learn the importance of confidentiality, integrity, and availability of data in mitigating information risks and emergent issues around data ethics.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS6024\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6024/2026-S1C-NE-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Managing Data at Scale\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Multiple weeks Closin