"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Using and Enhancing Data\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Workshop\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Data Quality Management\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Data Ethics\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Data Protection, Privacy, Security and Risks\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Data Governance and Maturity Assessment\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Group Project Workshop\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Group Presentations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Group Presentations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Analyse, identify, and define organisational data management challenges based on theory and practice\", \"LO2. Propose and justify data management strategies for real-world organisational scenarios\", \"LO3. Evaluate data-related systems, processes, components, and outcomes by applying theoretical concepts and knowledge of best practices\", \"LO4. Explain principles of data protection, privacy, security and data ethics\", \"LO5. Function effectively in a team to accomplish a common goal\", \"LO6. Communicate and engage orally and verbally in a professional manner\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"In-class assessment: Case studies will be assigned on a weekly basis and discussed in class.\", \"Case Studies: Case studies will be assigned on a weekly basis and discussed in class.\", \"Individual Assignment: Topic for this assignment will be provided by the lecturer and discussed in class.\", \"Group Report: The group report is part of the final project and will assess knowledge gained in all parts of the unit.\", \"Group Presentation: The group report is part of the final project and will assess abilities to communicate findings in oral and visual forms.\", \"Skills-based Assessment: Self-paced skill development exercises to be completed.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6024/2026-S1C-NE-CC\"]}'),(22,22,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.337052','Cybersecurity and Risk Management','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"None\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"This unit provides an in-depth understanding of cybersecurity management, essential to organisations in protecting their data and information assets against threats and attacks. Students are exposed to concepts, methodologies, and strategies that will equip them with key skills needed to prevent, detect, and mitigate security risks, while addressing ethical and privacy concerns. This unit covers a range of topics of cybersecurity risk management, including awareness of emerging technologies, information security policy, governance frameworks, and incident response planning in response to social engineering and ransomware attacks. Students also learn how to respond to and mitigate incidents and manage employees, while developing security education with focus on effective training and awareness programs.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS6026\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6026/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Cybersecurity and Risk Management\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Ongoing\", \"type\": \"Contribution\", \"length\": \"Weekly workshop contribution\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Class contribution Weekly workshop contribution\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 03 Due date: 21 Aug 2026 at 18:00 Closing date: 21 Aug 2026\", \"type\": \"Out-of-class quiz\", \"length\": \"10 minutes\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Early-Feedback Task Multiple-Choice Quiz\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 05 Due date: 31 Aug 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 11 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"500 words\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Reflective Report Reflective Report\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 07 Due date: 14 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 24 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"90 seconds\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Video briefing Video briefing\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 09 Due date: 06 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 16 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"1500 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Individual Report Individual Report\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 26 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 02 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"4500 words\", \"weight\": \"40%\", \"description\": \"Group project report Group Assignment\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 02 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 02 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"15 minutes\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Group presentation Group presentation\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.470372+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Cybersecurity Foundations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Cybersecurity Architecture (A)\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Cybersecurity Architecture (B)\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Incident Response\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Risk Management\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Assurance\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Governance, Strategy, and Privacy\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"AI in Cyber Attacks and Defense\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Group Project\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Group Project\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Industry Clinics for Group Project\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Incident Response (Advanced) and the Aftermath of Attacks\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Final Group Project Presentations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Discuss the role of information assets and governance management frameworks in protecting such assets in organisations\", \"LO2. Evaluate the risks associated with information assets and their impact on organisation\", \"LO3. Apply cybersecurity concepts and methodologies to design and implement effective security management strategies and practices\", \"LO4. Evaluate managerial approaches for addressing cybersecurity threats and attacks using real-world scenarios\", \"LO5. Address the impact of employee behaviours in mitigating security risks and ensuring a secure work environment\", \"LO6. Communicate cybersecurity issues, and collaborate towards solutions in a professional manner with people from diverse backgrounds\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6026/2026-S2C-ND-CC\"]}'),(23,23,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.370028','Agile Project Methods','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"None\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"Traditional approaches to project management and design work well under stable conditions, when intended outcomes are well understood. Yet, under conditional of market disruption, in innovation projects, new product development or for start-up businesses, traditional methods are often restrictive and inflexible. Agile Project Management and Design Thinking offer alternative approaches that value continuous change, flexibility, time-to-market, interactive learning and self-organisation over rigorous planning and design processes. In this unit, students will learn the ethos, principles, and methods of agile project management and design thinking. Students will experience hands-on techniques such as design thinking, learn management and Scrum as applied in practice. Learning will revolve around practical activities, insights from experienced guest speakers and case studies representing various industries.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS6032\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6032/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Agile Project Methods\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period Due date: 09 Jun 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 20 Jun 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"1500 words\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Essay Written essay\"}, {\"due\": \"Multiple weeks Closing date: 01 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"350 words\", \"weight\": \"20%\", \"description\": \"Learning journals Written journal\"}, {\"due\": \"Multiple weeks Closing date: 29 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Contribution\", \"length\": \"Multiple parts/times\", \"weight\": \"15%\", \"description\": \"Agile activities Participation\"}, {\"due\": \"Multiple weeks Closing date: 29 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Conversation\", \"length\": \"30 minutes\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Product owner meetings Discussion\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 22 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 05 Jun 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"Multiple media/parts/timing\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Agile project delivery Project delivery\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.530207+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction and overview\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Practices, principles and philosophy of agile project delivery\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Practices, principles and philosophy of design thinking\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Agile learning session 1\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Agile learning session 2\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Agile learning session 3\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Agile learning session 4\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Agile learning session 5\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Agile learning session 6\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Agile learning session 7\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Case study: TBA\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Case study: TBA\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Synthesis and wrap-up\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. demonstrate knowledge of the key principles of agile projects and their differences to traditional project management values\", \"LO2. describe and apply foundational agile project management practices\", \"LO3. describe and apply foundational design thinking practices\", \"LO4. reflect critically about key issues in agile project management and design thinking and communicate your reflections effectively\", \"LO5. engage effectively in team-based agile project management and design thinking practices\", \"LO6. communicate effectively in group work situations in accordance with agile project management and design thinking practices\", \"LO7. understand the principles of people-centric design and project work practices.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Learning journals: The purpose of the journal is to help students keep up to date with the required work and provide students and the unit coordinators with timely feedback on student learning progress. The journal is an integral part of the agile delivery of the core part of this unit. You will reflect on each week\'s topic utilising a set of brief instructions provided via Canvas for each of the learning journal submissions. Assessment of the weekly learning journal submissions will consider both grasps of the week\'s topic, but more importantly the degree of self-reflection and engagement with the topic.\", \"Agile activities: The agile learning sessions are built around hands-on activities whose learning outcomes depend on the active participation of each student. It is paramount that students pay attention to the purpose of each activity and show initiative in applying themselves to the task at hand. In-class activities involve the development of artefacts across agile sprints through iterative work, enabling multiple forms of engagement and active participation and allowing students to contribute in a variety of ways.\", \"Product owner meetings: The purpose of PO meetings is for students to engage in time-boxed discussions with a designated product owner from the teaching team to review each sprint’s MVP. Teams use targeted feedback on personas, design thinking, and concept development to refine their work and guide priorities for the next sprint, with an emphasis on learning and iteration rather than polished outcomes.\", \"Agile project delivery: The objective of the agile project is to use the knowledge and practical skills in agile projects and design thinking acquired in this unit of study to apply to a real-world problem. The focus of this project is in applying appropriate techniques and practices in creating the desired project outcome represented in diagrams, video and textual elements. While the outcome as such is not unimportant, the focus of this assignment and its assessment is on the application of the agile project management and design thinking techniques.\", \"Essay: The purpose of the essay is to demonstrate grasp of the agile philosophy and principles, as well as the agile project management and design thinking practices and techniques. The focus of the essay will lie in synthesising the learnings derived from the unit of study.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6032/2026-S1C-ND-CC\"]}'),(24,24,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.408216','Gadgets, Gods and Godzilla','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"INFS5002 or COMP5206\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"None\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"Technology is at the heart of many of the contemporary trends and challenges facing organisations. This unit of study introduces students to balanced and critical ways of thinking about the nature and role of technology in business and society. It problematizes the hype surrounding new technological developments (gadgets), the deification of technology as a solution to problems of all kinds (gods), and the, often unintended, consequences of technology applications (godzilla). The unit utilises current technological developments as examples for discussion and examines technology and its design and application in the context of social networks, power relations, and current business trends.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS6066\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6066/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Gadgets, Gods and Godzilla\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Week 02 Due date: 12 Aug 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 22 Aug 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"2.5 min video\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Reflection Journal The purpose of the reflection journal is to reflect on the weeks learning.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 04 Due date: 24 Aug 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 03 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"2.5 min video\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Reflection Journal The purpose of the reflection journal is to reflect on the weeks learning.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 06 Due date: 09 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 19 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"2.5 min video\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Reflection Journal The purpose of the reflection journal is to reflect on the weeks learning.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 08 Due date: 22 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 02 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Creative work\", \"length\": \"10 min exhibition including Q&A (in-clas\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Group Project Students will work in groups applying methods introduced during class.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 09 Due date: 09 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 19 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"1200 words; Written in pairs\", \"weight\": \"40%\", \"description\": \"Final Essay The final report/essay will provide opportunity to synthesize learning.\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.540730+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Futures, Megatrends and Metaphors\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Forecasting, Prediction; Gadgets on the Loose\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Scenario thinking, scenario analysis; Modern Gods\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Foresight, speculative futures ; Monsters in the Classroom\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Digital Geographies; Speculation\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"AI Fluency Sprint\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Technological Imaginaries\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"In-class work and Presentations; Attendance Mandatory\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Make and defend evaluations of socio-technical systems.\", \"LO2. Construct arguments regarding the benefits and consequences of technologies in organizational and societal setting.\", \"LO3. Categorize perspectives based on alternative ethical stances.\", \"LO4. Create and assess alternative scenario prototypes of socio-technical systems at individual, organizational and societal levels.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Video learning journals (working in pairs, (3 videos 2.5 minutes each; via Canvas)\", \"Group project presentations/demonstrations in class (10 minutes exhibition including Q&A during week 8. 750 word writeup due same day.)\", \"Final essay (1000 words) submitted via Canvas\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6066/2026-S2C-ND-CC\"]}'),(25,25,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.434479','Project Management in Business','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"None\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"This unit introduces students to the foundations of project management in business contexts. It provides an overview and practical understanding of different project management approaches, ranging from structured frameworks such as the Project Management Body of Knowledge (PMBOK) to light-weight techniques such as Agile or Lean Management. Students learn how to select appropriate projects based on their alignment with an organisation\'s strategy and then how to manage those projects successfully from initiation through execution to completion. The unit explores both the technical and behavioural aspects of project management and students gain experience in critically analysing the application of techniques in specific business contexts. As organisations increasingly structure their activities on a project basis, the unit is of value to a range of discipline specialisations.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS6071\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6071/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Project Management in Business\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period Due date: 16 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 26 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"2,000 words\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Individual assignment 2 Written task\"}, {\"due\": \"Multiple weeks\", \"type\": \"In-person written or creative task\", \"length\": \"30 min to 1 hour per tutorial.\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Individual assignment 1 Written tasks completed in tutorials\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 04 Due date: 24 Aug 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 28 Aug 2026\", \"type\": \"Out-of-class quiz\", \"length\": \"20 minutes\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Class Quiz Online quiz\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 30 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 09 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"3,000 words\", \"weight\": \"35%\", \"description\": \"Group Project Assignment Written task\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.566685+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"The Project Management Landscape [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"The Project Management Landscape [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Value, Strategy and Benefits Realisation [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Value, Strategy and Benefits Realisation [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Principles, Ethics and Governance [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Principles, Ethics and Governance [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Complexity, Uncertainty and Project Context [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Complexity, Uncertainty and Project Context [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Project Life Cycles and Delivery Approaches [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Project Life Cycles and Delivery Approaches [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Scope, Planning and Risk Fundamentals [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Scope, Planning and Risk Fundamentals [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Risk, Uncertainty and Project Challenges [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Risk, Uncertainty and Project Challenges [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Communication and Stakeholder Engagement [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Communication and Stakeholder Engagement [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Resources, Teams and Leadership [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Resources, Teams and Leadership [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Performance, Finance and Benefits Realisation [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Performance, Finance and Benefits Realisation [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Sustainability, Responsibility and Emerging Challenges [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Sustainability, Responsibility and Emerging Challenges [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Tailoring, Adaptive and Hybrid Project Management [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Tailoring, Adaptive and Hybrid Project Management [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"AI, PM Maturity and Professional Identity [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"AI, PM Maturity and Professional Identity [online recorded lecture + on-campus tutorial]\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Apply and analyse project management methodologies, frameworks and knowledge areas for planning across the project lifecycle.\", \"LO2. Evaluate formal and adaptive project management practices, considering their guiding principles and processes.\", \"LO3. Evaluate and critically analyse the factors affecting various project stakeholders and team processes.\", \"LO4. Create and critically reflect on project planning by applying relevant project management techniques.\", \"LO5. Identify and critically assess project challenges, success factors and the applicability of formal project management frameworks.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Class Quiz: One (1) online quiz will be completed by Monday, Week 4 at 23:59. The quiz assesses students’ understanding of foundational project management concepts introduced in the first three weeks of the unit, including the project management landscape, project value and benefits, governance, ethics, professional practice, and contemporary project management frameworks. Questions may draw on lecture content, tutorial activities, prescribed readings, and other learning resources. The quiz will be administered through Canvas using a variety of question types. Students will be assessed on the accuracy and completeness of their responses.\", \"Individual Assignment 1: This secure written assessment is completed progressively during tutorials in Weeks 4, 5 and 6. Students will assume the role of a project team member who has been asked by a Project Manager to investigate a proposed project and provide professional advice to support project planning and decision-making.\", \"Working with a supplied project scenario, students will engage with project information and stakeholder perspectives to analyse the project’s context, complexity, uncertainty, and organisational environment. Students will evaluate the suitability of different project management approaches, identify project risks and challenges, and provide recommendations regarding project delivery, planning priorities, and success factors.\", \"This assessment is completed during scheduled tutorial classes. Work undertaken during each tutorial session will be recorded to maintain assessment integrity and combined into a final submission. Students will be assessed on their ability to critically analyse project contexts, evaluate project management frameworks and methodologies, identify project challenges, and provide well-supported recommendations for project planning and delivery.\", \"Group Project Assignment: This assignment adopts a Project-Based Learning (PBL) approach, in which student teams act as a project planning team, developing a comprehensive project planning portfolio for a simulated, realistic organisational project.\", \"Throughout the semester, teams will work within a simulated project environment supported by AI-enabled stakeholder personas. These stakeholder personas may represent, for example, project sponsors, clients, regulators, technical specialists, end users, operations managers, and other project stakeholders. Teams will be required to actively engage with these stakeholders to gather information, clarify requirements, resolve conflicts, manage emerging issues, and inform project planning decisions.\", \"As the project progresses, new information, stakeholder feedback, constraints, and project challenges may emerge, requiring teams to reassess assumptions and adapt their planning approaches. Students will apply project management concepts, methodologies, frameworks, and techniques to develop a professional project planning portfolio that may include governance structures, project scope, methodology selection and tailoring rationale, schedules, stakeholder and communication plans, resource and team plans, risk management artefacts, and approaches to measuring project success and benefits realisation.\", \"Students will be expected to justify their project management decisions and demonstrate how project context, complexity, uncertainty, stakeholder needs, and organisational objectives have influenced their chosen approach. Students will be assessed on the quality and integration of their planning artefacts, continued and professional engagement with project stakeholders, application of project management theory to practice, and the strength of their supporting rationale.\", \"Individual Assignment 2: This individual assessment requires students to critically evaluate project management decisions made throughout the group project and reflect on their professional learning and development. Drawing on unit concepts, contemporary project management literature, experiences gained through the project simulation, and interactions with project stakeholders, students will prepare a concise analytical report examining the suitability and application of project management approaches in complex organisational environments.\", \"Students will critically analyse project challenges, stakeholder dynamics, team processes, planning decisions, and the selection and tailoring of project management methodologies and frameworks. Students will also reflect on lessons learned from the project, considering how project complexity, uncertainty, stakeholder engagement, and emerging technologies influenced project outcomes and decision-making.\", \"Students will be assessed on the quality of their critical analysis, the integration of relevant literature, the depth of their reflection, their ability to evaluate project management approaches, and their capacity to justify recommendations for future practice.\", \"Detailed instructions, marking criteria, project briefs, stakeholder interaction requirements, and submission guidelines for all assessments will be provided on Canvas.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6071/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6071/2026-S2C-ND-CC\"]}'),(26,26,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.487500','Digital, Data and Decisions','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"None\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"Despite the widely acknowledged importance of data as a strategic resource, organisations continue to struggle with appropriately managing their data assets. This unit highlights the crucial importance of data governance in the administration, protection, and optimisation of data. It provides an exploration into the core principles of data governance, discusses the influence of emerging technologies, such as artificial intelligence, and examines the broader societal and ethical implications surrounding data. Through interactive case studies and hands-on assessments, students learn about the complex issues surrounding data governance and formulate and persuasively communicate practical data governance approaches to empower organisations to navigate their data landscape.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS6077\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6077/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Digital, Data and Decisions\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Week 06 Due date: 11 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 18 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"1500 word report.\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Individual Report A Please see detailed Individual Assignment requirements in Canvas.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 08 Due date: 25 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 02 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"750-word report\", \"weight\": \"0%\", \"description\": \"Individual report B Part I Report B part I (750 words); marked for completeness. Failure to submit will affect the mark for Report B part II.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 10 Due date: 16 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 23 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"1500 words report + attendance w9.\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Individual Report B Part II Full report after a mandatory in-class assignment in week 9. Failure to attend in class in week 9 will prohibit the student from being able to submit this report. Further assignment requirements in Canvas.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 03 Nov 2026 at 17:00 Closing date: 03 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"10 minute pitch\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Group project pitch Pitch of data strategy\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 06 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 13 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"4000 words\", \"weight\": \"40%\", \"description\": \"Group project report Full report with formative submissions throughout the semester. See Canvas for full details.\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.600555+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction and overview\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Introduction to data governance\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Strategies, programs, and frameworks\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Data quality\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"The impact of emerging technologies\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Leveraging digital data for decision-making\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Societal and ethical considerations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Securing and protecting data\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Collective data governance\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Power and politics\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Managing and sustaining change\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Guest lecture, review and synthesis\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Group pitches\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Discuss and apply the foundational principles and concepts of data governance, including stewardship, data quality, security, metadata, and lifecycle management\", \"LO2. Reflect critically on the implications of emerging technologies, such as artificial intelligence, blockchain, and internet of things, on data governance practices\", \"LO3. Analyze case studies of data governance practices in various (inter)organizational contexts to identify stakeholders, key success factors, challenges, and opportunities\", \"LO4. Evaluate the broader societal and ethical implications surrounding data governance, including privacy and transparency, and propose and discuss the effectiveness of ethical guidelines\", \"LO5. Assess how organizations can leverage digital data and become more data-driven to enhance decision-making processes\", \"LO6. Develop and present a comprehensive data governance strategy tailored to a specific case organization, integrating lecture materials, and ensuring alignment with the organizational context\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Group project report and pitch: The group project is an applied assignment that requires the team\'s development of a data strategy.\", \"Individual Assignment A: The report will assess the implications of generative AI for data governance.\", \"Individual Assignment B: The report will explore decision-making with data in complex organizational settings.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6077/2026-S2C-ND-CC\"]}'),(27,27,'archive-import-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:40.520055','Business Information Systems Capstone','{\"level\": \"PG\", \"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"Completion of 6 credit points towards the Digital Business or Business Information Systems specialisation\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"Completion of 18 credit points of units towards the Digital Business or Business Information Systems specialisation (including INFS5002)\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"None\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"No\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal evening] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"This unit bridges the gap between theory and practice by integrating the knowledge and key skills developed across the Digital Business or Business Information Systems specialisation. It provides students with a practical teamwork experience to identify, analyse, and solve contemporary problems by exploring the socio-technical ecosystem in which a digital business operates. This experiential learning opportunity enhances students’ career-readiness by consolidating their professional skills and ability to apply the Business Information Systems knowledge in authentic situations.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS6600\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6600/2026-S2C-NE-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Business Information Systems Capstone\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period\", \"type\": \"Written exam\", \"length\": \"1.5 hours\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Leadership and Professional Development: Goals Achievement Overall assessment of the goals achievement\"}, {\"due\": \"Multiple weeks\", \"type\": \"Q&A following presentation, submission or placement\", \"length\": \"3 mins\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Project Progress Q&A QA&A post project progress presentation\"}, {\"due\": \"Multiple weeks\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"3 minutes\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Project Progress Infographic submission Project progress updates presentation\"}, {\"due\": \"Multiple weeks\", \"type\": \"Evaluation\", \"length\": \"Up to 8 mins / submission\", \"weight\": \"0%\", \"description\": \"Groupwork Accountability Survey Peer review to assess members contribution and determine their group marks\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 02 Due date: 16 Aug 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 26 Aug 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"Up to 2 pages submission\", \"weight\": \"3%\", \"description\": \"Group Charter Each group must establish roles and norms.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 03 Due date: 23 Aug 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 02 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"Up to 5 slides\", \"weight\": \"7%\", \"description\": \"Business Models Submission of partner\'s business, operating models, and IS architecture\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 04 Due date: 30 Aug 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 09 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Practical skill\", \"length\": \"Up to 1000 words\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Leadership and Professional Development: Personal Goals Personal goals development plan\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 05 Due date: 02 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 02 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"5 mins presentation + 5 mins Q&A\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Pitch to Partner Slides submission before class Delivered during class time\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 08 Due date: 27 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 07 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Creative work\", \"length\": \"Up to 3 pages\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Innovation Review Submission of Innovation Scorecard and infographic of solution idea\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 28 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 28 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"5 min presentation\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Boardroom Presentation Slides submission before class Live presentation\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 28 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 28 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Q&A following presentation, submission or placement\", \"length\": \"5 mins\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Boardroom Presentation Q&A Q&A post boardroom presentation\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 08 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 15 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"Up to 3,000 words\", \"weight\": \"20%\", \"description\": \"Group Report Written report\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-01T03:39:02.667129+00:00\", \"selection_rule\": \"Prefer Semester 2; otherwise Semester 1; otherwise first published 2026 outline.\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Unit Introduction & Project Immersion\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Partner Briefing & Group Collaboration\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Problem & Opportunity Scoping\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Project Planning\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Pitch to Partner\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Research & Analysis\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Ideation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Prototyping\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Prototype Validation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Implementation Planning\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Presentation Planning\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Boardroom Presentation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Review & Closure\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Apply advanced principles and theories related to digital business and information systems in the context of an actual business organisation\", \"LO2. Analyse open-ended business scenarios and evaluate the implications of digital technologies for business\", \"LO3. Apply techniques for organisational and business process analyses to resolve an actual business problem\", \"LO4. Communicate in a professional and confident manner, both orally and in writing, with actual business professionals\", \"LO5. Apply team management concepts and theories to effectively work in, and manage, cross-functional teams\", \"LO6. Analyse and reflect critically on the ethical and social implications of digital business\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Group Charter - submisison of a group project charter including group roles and norms.\", \"Business Models - submission of partner\'s business business and operating models, and IS architecture.\", \"Pitch to Partner - presentation of a compelling case for problem/ opportunity and a project plan.Collection of feedback from partner, academics, and peers.\", \"Innovation Review - submission of Innovation scorecard (template) and infographic of solution idea, to ensure innovative and creative thinking is embedded in the final solution.\", \"Project Progress and Q&A – individual presentation followed by Q&A by each member of the team on the project progress as well as insights and lessons learned on professional skills.\", \"Group Report – detailed report clearly communicating the project background, approach, analyses,solution recommendation and implementation.\", \"Boardroom Presentation and Q&A - presentation of the project work with focus on solution recommendation followed by Q&A.\", \"Leadership and Professional Development – exploration, development, and reflection on leadership and professional skills through the development of a portfolio for job applications, career development, and workplace success (professional identity). Two assessments: 1) Personal Goals development plan, and 2) Goals Achievement final exam, which is a hurdle task - students must pass it in order to pass the unit. Failing the hurdle task results in a capped fail mark of 49 in the unit.\"], \"available_2026_outlines\": [\"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6600/2026-S1C-NE-CC\", \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS6600/2026-S2C-NE-CC\"]}'),(28,28,'outline-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:41.784181','Digital Business Innovation','{\"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"ISYS1003 or INFO1000\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"None\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"The Digital Economy, with its focus on information as a key business resource, has changed the way Business Information Systems (BIS) is viewed in organisations. BIS are now seen as enablers of innovation in which people, supported by powerful technology, are considered to be the most important component. This is because problem-solving, innovation and critical thinking skills cannot be outsourced or easily acquired by competitors. This unit is designed to develop your understanding of how businesses operate. It shows how information systems support business operations and management through integration of people, business processes and systems. You will be provided with an introduction to state-of-the-art business analysis techniques, frameworks and models to assist in understanding the nature and contribution of BIS in a range of business contexts. With its emphasis on business rather than IT, this unit does not require prior IT-related experience. In this unit, you will learn about the increasingly important role of IT in business and acquire valuable business analysis and problem-solving skills.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS1000\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS1000/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Digital Business Innovation\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period\", \"type\": \"Written exam\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"35%\", \"description\": \"Final exam Written exam\"}, {\"due\": \"Multiple weeks\", \"type\": \"Practical skill\", \"length\": \"n/a\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Weekly Homework Practical task\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 03\", \"type\": \"Out-of-class quiz\", \"length\": \"10-minutes\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Early Feedback Task #EarlyFeedbackTask\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 07 Due date: 19 Apr 2026 at 15:40\", \"type\": \"Written test\", \"length\": \"1 hour\", \"weight\": \"20%\", \"description\": \"Mid-semester exam Written exam\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 22 May 2026 at 17:00 Closing date: 29 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"3000 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Group project Project report\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-11T04:52:41.782295+00:00\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Unit overview and introduction to information systems\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Introduction to business processes\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Introduction to business processes\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Strategy and competitive advantage\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Strategy and competitive advantage\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Databases and information design\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO4 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Databases and information design\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO4 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Data modelling\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO4 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Data modelling\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO4 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Business information systems ethics\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Business information systems ethics\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Business process management\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Business process management\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Business process modelling (BPMN)\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Business process modelling (BPMN)\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Business process analysis and improvement\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Business process analysis and improvement\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Project consultation\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Project consultation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Cybersecurity\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Cybersecurity\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Business intelligence systems\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Business intelligence systems\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Exam briefing and unit review\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO6\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. make judgments about ways in which Information Technology can enable innovation and improvement in business\", \"LO2. demonstrate basic business analysis skills, such as strategic analysis, data modelling, and business process modelling\", \"LO3. create and understand visual representations of business processes using BPMN notation, as well as basic data models as the basis for information systems design\", \"LO4. create databases in MS Access software\", \"LO5. understand and address basic issues pertaining to information ethics\", \"LO6. work as an effective team in a problem-solving environment\", \"LO7. display an understanding of business design in an end-to-end manner from business idea to business execution.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Group work: Team-based learning (TBL) is a set of group-based activities designed to help students to acquire higher-level learning skills in this context, including creativity, lateral and reflective thinking, problem-solving and critical analysis as well as the applied and experiential BIS knowledge. This assessment component will consider group homework preparation for the TBL sessions as well as their work and participation in, and outcomes of collaborative in-class activities in finalising their homework submissions. The TBL activity sheets will be uploaded on Canvas.\", \"Early Feedback Task: The objective of this task is to focus on the fundamental concepts, and applied knowledge acquired in sessions 1-3. This task will be in the form of a Canvas Quiz with multiple choice questions.\", \"Group project report: The objective of this task is to use the knowledge and practical skills acquired in this unit of study to investigate and solve a real-life business problem. Students are expected to create and describe their own business idea for a pre-defined scenario, which they will receive through Canvas.\", \"Mid-semester exam: This exam will focus on the fundamental concepts, and applied and experiential knowledge acquired in weeks 1-6 of the unit. Note: This assessment will take the form of a supervised exam. It is a closed-book exam with no materials allowed.\", \"Final exam: This exam will focus on the fundamental concepts, and applied and experiential knowledge acquired in weeks 1-12, including the practical assignment. Note: This assessment will take the form of a supervised exam. It is a closed-book exam with no materials allowed.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"]}'),(29,29,'outline-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:42.319179','Digital Business Innovation','{\"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"ISYS1003 or INFO1000\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"None\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"The Digital Economy, with its focus on information as a key business resource, has changed the way Business Information Systems (BIS) is viewed in organisations. BIS are now seen as enablers of innovation in which people, supported by powerful technology, are considered to be the most important component. This is because problem-solving, innovation and critical thinking skills cannot be outsourced or easily acquired by competitors. This unit is designed to develop your understanding of how businesses operate. It shows how information systems support business operations and management through integration of people, business processes and systems. You will be provided with an introduction to state-of-the-art business analysis techniques, frameworks and models to assist in understanding the nature and contribution of BIS in a range of business contexts. With its emphasis on business rather than IT, this unit does not require prior IT-related experience. In this unit, you will learn about the increasingly important role of IT in business and acquire valuabl