s Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"INFS1000 or INFS1020 or INFO1000 or INFO1003 or INFO1903\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"The rapid evolution and widespread adoption of digital technologies are transforming societies, economies, and the environment. These transformations bring significant benefits but also pose serious risks of harm, particularly for vulnerable and marginalised stakeholders, requiring a balanced approach that genuinely serves the common good. This unit unpacks the ethical dilemmas of sociotechnical change, exploring how digital technologies like artificial intelligence, blockchain, and social media impact privacy, equity, democracy, dignity, business, the environment, and more. Guided by compassion as a foundational principle, students will critically examine the ambiguous and pluralistic nature of digital innovations, developing the skills to manage ethical dilemmas and promote responsible digital innovation for the common good. The course integrates ethical theories with recent practical case studies, equipping students to address contemporary ethical dilemmas in today\'s digital world.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS2010\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS2010/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Digital Ethics and Compassionate Innovation\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Week 03 Due date: 19 Aug 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 13 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"600 words\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"GenAI Strategy Plan and critically reflect on using GenAI and how it affects learning.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 06 Due date: 09 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 30 Sep 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"1500 words\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Individual assignment Written report\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 30 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 30 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Interactive oral\", \"length\": \"3 slides / 5 minutes\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Group Assignment / Business Presentation Slides and Oral presentation\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 06 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 30 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"1500 words\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Reflective Journal Individual reflective learning journal, stimulated by prompts.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 06 Nov 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 30 Nov 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"3000 words\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Group Assignment / Business Report Written report\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-11T04:52:44.279433+00:00\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Unit introduction\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Warm-Up & Individual Assignment Prep\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"The Big Picture: Compassionate Digital Innovation\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Selecting Evidence\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Digital Harm Analysis\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Analysing Evidence\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Ambivalent Affordances of Digital Technologies\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Privacy vs Security\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Ethical Paradoxes of Sociotechnical Change\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Group Assignment Prep\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Compassion: A Timely and Timeless Moral Guide\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Bust Your Own Bias\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Case Study: Responsible Social Media Use\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Designing Responsibility Nudges\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Case Study: Compassionate Web3 Governance\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Presenting Evidence\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Case Study: Human-AI Companionship\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Sensitive Issues in Sociotechnical Systems\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Developing Employability\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Developing Your Employability\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Open Topic / Guest Lecture\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Reflection on common issues in assignments\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Unit Review and Debrief\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Group Project Presentations\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Open Topic / Guest Lecture\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Reflection Workshop\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Analyse the ethical challenges and paradoxes associated with digital innovations.\", \"LO2. Critically evaluate the societal, economic, and environmental impacts of digital technologies, considering both benefits and harms.\", \"LO3. Apply ethical frameworks and theories to navigate and resolve ethical dilemmas in digital innovation.\", \"LO4. Communicate and collaborate effectively on ethical issues in diverse professional contexts.\", \"LO5. Make informed decisions that balance technological progress with societal well-being and ethical integrity.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Individual assignment: This assignment aims to assist students’ development as an independent learner of the knowledge and skills acquired in this unit. Students are to select, analyse and submit a brief business report on a news item for a specific company relevant to this unit.\", \"Group project report: The group project is an applied project aimed at assessing the knowledge acquired in this unit of study. Students will be required to demonstrate their critical thinking and analytical skills to analyse and recommend improvements to real-world businesses.\", \"Group project presentation: The group project presentations are aimed at assessing the level of knowledge acquired in this unit of study. All group members must actively participate in the presentation.\", \"Reflective journal: Individual continuous, reflective journal about the student’s authentic learning experience gleaned from the group project and the in-class activities. While the submission is due in W13, students are encouraged to reflect on weekly prompts by the teaching team, and can discuss their reflections in the weekly consultations. The final deliverable is a written reflective journal of 1500 words (± 10%).\", \"Generative AI Strategy: By developing a clear strategy for using GenAI tools in assignment work, students will enhance their ability to leverage GenAI for tasks such as identifying literature, structuring ideas, refining text, and fact-checking while ensuring ethical and effective practices. The goal is not just to ensure academic integrity but also to reflect on how GenAI tools shape analytical and critical thinking skills. The deliverable is a 600 word (± 10%) document.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"]}'),(33,33,'outline-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:45.215458','Digital Business Management','{\"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"INFS1000 or INFO1000 or INFO1003 or INFO1903\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"This unit will provide you with a detailed overview of the concepts and models used in doing business digitally via the Internet. These concepts and models will enable you to evaluate, synthesise and implement Internet-enabled business models. The unit will provide the critical link between the firm\'s performance and modern Internet technologies, such as e-Commerce platforms, Social Media and Social Networking. Emphasis will be put on the utilisation of Internet technologies to enable new forms of digital business, rather than on the technologies themselves. Assumed knowledge for this unit is INFS1000 or equivalent.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS2030\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS2030/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Digital Business Management\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period\", \"type\": \"Written exam\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"50%\", \"description\": \"Final exam Written exam with MCQ\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 03 Due date: 10 Mar 2026 at 14:00 Closing date: 10 Mar 2026\", \"type\": \"Out-of-class quiz\", \"length\": \"30 minutes\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Early feedback quiz MCQ Early Feedback Task\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 06 Due date: 31 Mar 2026 at 14:00 Closing date: 31 Mar 2026\", \"type\": \"In-class quiz\", \"length\": \"30 minutes\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Mid-semester Quiz MCQ\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 18 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 28 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"2000 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Group project report Written report\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 19 May 2026 at 13:00 Closing date: 27 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"10 minutes\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Group project presentation Oral presentation\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-11T04:52:45.213703+00:00\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction and unit overview\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction and unit overview\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Digital economy\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Digital economy\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Digital business models\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Digital business models\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Digital strategy\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Digital strategy\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Digital marketing\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Digital marketing\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Big-data\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Digital marketing\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Using AI in digital business\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Using AI in digital business\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"AI ethics\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"AI ethics\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Design of Digital Systems & Usability\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Design of Digital Systems & Usability\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Innovation and digital disruption\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Innovation and digital disruption\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Review\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Review\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Group assignment presentations\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Group assignment presentations\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Group assignment presentations\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Group assignment presentations\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. identify and analyse major current trends in conducting digital business and understand the key challenges in developing and managing businesses in online contexts\", \"LO2. diagnose and solve business problems and design business solutions that can result in significant business improvement through the exploitation of information and communications technologies\", \"LO3. perform as effective team members in a variety of business contexts and act in a professional business manner as both members and leaders of teams, and in relation to client interactions\", \"LO4. communicate a business plan in a clear and convincing manner both written and verbal\", \"LO5. identify market opportunities and develop strategies to take advantage of them.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Early feedback quiz: This quiz will be based on the content delivered during weeks 1-2 of the semester. Students are required to attempt all questions.\", \"Mid-semester exam: This exam will be based on the content delivered during weeks 3-5 of the semester. Students are required to attempt all questions.\", \"Group project report: The group project is an applied assignment that involves the development of a business plan for a new product or service of your choice. You will outline the value proposition, define the key components of the digital business model, analyse competitive positioning, articulate the digital business capabilities required, and develop an implementation plan. Groups will submit a report that captures these elements.\", \"Group project presentation: Each group will present its business plan during the lectures and tutorials in weeks 12 and 13. All group members must participate in the presentation.\", \"Final exam: This is a closed-book exam that will cover material from all weekly classes and readings. This exam will consist of an extended answer, case study and multiple-choice questions.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"]}'),(34,34,'outline-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:46.049295','Working in High-Performing Project Teams','{\"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"INFO3402 or ENGG1850\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"INFS1000 or INFS1020 or INFO1000 or INFO1003 or INFO1903\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"Projects are a common way of managing organisational transformation and change, the development of new products and the implementation of Information Technology (IT) in business. Information Systems (IS) business analysts will work in projects and need an understanding of both project management and the project environment. This unit will introduce you to the end-to-end project management lifecycle as described in the Project Management Body of Knowledge (PMBOK). You will learn how to successfully manage projects from initiation through execution to completion. The focus of this unit will be on the management, execution, and coordination of project activities. To this end, you will learn hands-on project management techniques and gain first-hand experience with a modern online project management platform, including an introduction to agile project management methodologies.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS2040\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS2040/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Working in High-Performing Project Teams\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period\", \"type\": \"Written exam\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"50%\", \"description\": \"Final exam n/a\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 03 Due date: 15 Aug 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 22 Aug 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"600 words\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Project Guide Early feedback\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 26 Oct 2025 at 23:59 Closing date: 02 Nov 2025\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"n/a\", \"weight\": \"40%\", \"description\": \"Group assignment Group assignment around the case study and the team\'s approach\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-11T04:52:46.047230+00:00\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction to project management and teams\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"The project management lifecycles + CX -UX -UI: I have to do what exactly? Is this the right thing to even be doing?\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Agile and procedural management and the role of the project manager : So you want to run this thing?\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"The triple constraint and scope management: we are doing what exactly?\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Cost and resource management: how much is this going to be?\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Risk management: do you feel lucky?\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Time and people management: how to plan and how to motivate\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Scheduling teams and resolving disputes: hard and soft PM skills\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Adaptive and agile management: a different way of working\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Adaptive and agile management: At scale DevOps\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Procurement & Stakeholder Management - what resources do I need and how do I communicate with stakeholders? Resolving disputes\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Quality and communications management: getting it right internally and externally\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Unit review. Best way to pass the exam!\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. describe the key phases of the project management life cycle including the tools and methods used in each of those phases\", \"LO2. describe and explain the need for project management in the context of information systems (IS)\", \"LO3. recognise the key attributes of alternative IS project management methodologies including Agile methods\", \"LO4. analyse critically past Information Systems projects, explaining and justifying key themes that contributed to the success or failure of those projects\", \"LO5. set up and manage a project using an online project management platform\", \"LO6. describe and explain contemporary IS trends and issues that impact IS project management\", \"LO7. learn about design thinking and how to manage ideas in a team setting, both hard and soft skills.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Group assignment: Utilising an online project management platform, groups are required to organise their work and develop a report relating to specific information systems (IS) project management issue or trend. Students will be provided a list of contemporary IS project management trends/issues in week 2. Each group must select one of these topics and submit their nominated topic for approval to the unit coordinator by week 4. No more than three groups may select the same issue/trend.\", \"Project Guide: Individual conceptualisation of the nature of projects both formation and execution as a written individual submission.\", \"Written exam. A final assessment that pulls together the concepts and implications of project development and management. This is focused on concepts as discussed in the lectures, not defintions. Importantly this builds on both lecture material and your group work.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"]}'),(35,35,'outline-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:46.795819','Data Governance and Technology Assurance','{\"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"INFS3010 or INFS3030\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"INFS1000 or INFO1000 or INFO1003 or INFO1903\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"Data governance is a major imperative for organisations in effectively managing, using, protecting and leveraging their critical data assets. This unit introduces students to key concepts, processes, technologies and stakeholders related to the design and implementation of a data governance program. The unit takes an interdisciplinary and multi-level approach that examines standards, frameworks and methodologies for managing data quality, protecting critical and sensitive information, supporting business analytics and meeting compliance obligations. In examining different stages of the data lifecycle, students also learn about legal, professional and ethical responsibilities, policy implications, required skill sets and accountabilities.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS2050\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS2050/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Data Governance and Technology Assurance\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Week 03 Due date: 13 Mar 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 23 Mar 2026\", \"type\": \"Contribution\", \"length\": \"2 minutes presentation; 2 posts\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Personal Learning Objectives Presentation and responses to presentations by others.\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 06 Due date: 02 Apr 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 16 Apr 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"1500 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Individual Assignment One Written task\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 18 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 18 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"2000 words\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Group Assignment Group Report Submission\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 18 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 18 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"-\", \"weight\": \"6%\", \"description\": \"Group Assignment: Presentation Group Presentation\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 22 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 22 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Evaluation\", \"length\": \"-\", \"weight\": \"4%\", \"description\": \"Group Assignment: Individual Evaluation Individual Evaluation\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 29 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 12 Jun 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"1800 words\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Individual Assignment Two Written task\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-11T04:52:46.794241+00:00\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction: The Data Governance Imperative\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction: The Data Governance Imperative\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Data governance: definitions, principles and frameworks (Part 1)\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Data governance: definitions, principles and frameworks (Part 1)\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Data governance: definitions, principles and frameworks (Part 2)\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Data governance: definitions, principles and frameworks (Part 2)\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Directed data, surveillance and governance\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Directed data, surveillance and governance\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Volunteered data, analytics and governance\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Volunteered data, analytics and governance\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Self Directed Learning and Consultation\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Self directed learning and consultation\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Automated data, platforms and governance\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Automated data, platforms and governance\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Data ethics and data governance\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Data ethics and data governance\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Data integrity and data governance\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Data integrity and data governance\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Data security and data governance\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Data security and data governance\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Self directed learning and consultation\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Self directed learning and consultation\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Group Presentation and Evaluation\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Group Presentation and Evaluation\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Data Governance Evaluation and Application\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Data Governance Evaluation and Application\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (1.5 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6 LO7\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. describe key concepts and principles of data governance and explain their importance in business information systems\", \"LO2. demonstrate knowledge of the relationship of data governance with security, privacy, ethics and regulatory functions\", \"LO3. identify key stakeholders involved in governing data in different and changing institutional contexts and technology platforms\", \"LO4. demonstrate knowledge of key data governance standards and frameworks and practise their application through class activities and assigned tasks\", \"LO5. conduct research to develop and support arguments about key issues, challenges and trends associated with data governance in business\", \"LO6. communicate in oral and written form your knowledge, thoughts and findings through class discussions, group work and individual assignments\", \"LO7. provide constructive feedback to your peers on their written work, and address issues identified by your instructor and peers when reflecting and revising your own written work.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"The assessments support the learning objectives of the unit.\", \"Individual Assignment One: This assignment will require you to integrate information from lectures and tutorials to create a concise written report.\", \"Individual Assignment Two: This assignment will require you to integrate information from lectures and tutorials to create a concise written report.\", \"Group Assignment: This assignment consists of a presentation as well as other written and non-written elements.\", \"Personal Learning Objectives (PLO): In week 3 you will present your PLO for this unit and respond to the PLO of your peers.\", \"Submission links and more information about each assessment are available on Canvas.\"]}'),(36,36,'outline-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:47.282305','Enterprise Systems and Integrated Business','{\"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"INFS1000 or INFO1000 or INFO1003 or INFO1903\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 2, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"This unit provides students with an in-depth understanding of the way in which implementation and use of large scale integrated Enterprise Systems change the nature of organisational capabilities, processes, and roles. Students will gain an understanding of the strategic role of Enterprise Systems in providing a platform for improved business operations and designing information infrastructures. In this unit, students will gain considerable hands-on experience with an enterprise-wide system, such as SAP, concentrating on the way in which such systems support integrated business processes. Through a combination of discussion and practical work, students will gain a strong knowledge in both the organisational and technical aspects of Enterprise Systems. Students will also explore the emergence and implications of cloud-based Enterprise Systems and the implementation process. Assumed knowledge for this unit is INFS1000 or equivalent.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS3040\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3040/2026-S2C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Enterprise Systems and Integrated Business\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period\", \"type\": \"Written exam\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"50%\", \"description\": \"Final Exam n/a\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 08 Due date: 25 Sep 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 02 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"1000 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Individual assignment n/a\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 11 Due date: 23 Oct 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 28 Oct 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"2500 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Group project n/a\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-11T04:52:47.280859+00:00\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction to Business Processes and Enterprise Systems\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Financial accounting 1\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Financial accounting 2\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Procurement 1\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Procurement 2\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"CATWOE 1 & Enterprise Systems and Information Infrastructures\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"CATWOE 2 & Software Vendor Evaluation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"ES Trends – Cloud Applications\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Managing ES – Risks and Controls\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Managing ES Projects\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Group Assignment Work and Consultations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Group Presentations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO7\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Review and Preparation for the Final Exam\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. demonstrate your understanding of the integrated nature of Enterprise Systems and their strategic role in providing a platform for improved business operations\", \"LO2. demonstrate your understanding of the role of Enterprise Systems in information infrastructures and evaluate the organisational effects of increasing integration and standardisation\", \"LO3. demonstrate your understanding of the issues associated with implementing and supporting Enterprise Systems.\", \"LO4. demonstrate your understanding of the change undergone in organisations during the implementation and use of Enterprise Systems\", \"LO5. demonstrate your theoretical understanding of the organisational structures, master data and processes within SAP\", \"LO6. demonstrate your hands-on experience in executing transactions and analysing information using SAP\", \"LO7. work both independently and as a responsible member of a diverse team, collaborate and communicate in a professional manner with people from diverse backgrounds.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Individual assignment: Based upon a business case and your own independent research, you are required to complete hands-on exercises in SAP and design an SAP organisational structure for an implementation project.\", \"Group project: Based upon a business case and the group\'s independent research, students are required to analyse an information infrastructure and identify requirements for an Enterprise Systems solution.\", \"Final exam: This is a closed book exam. Students will need to demonstrate their understanding of all material covered in the unit of study.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"]}'),(37,37,'outline-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:47.814306','Business Intelligence for Managers','{\"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"None\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"INFS1000 or INFO1000 or INFO1003 or INFO1903\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"To gain or maintain their competitive edge, more than ever before, organisations need to rely on high-quality information to support decision making processes at all organisational levels. Business Intelligence (BI) is now being recognised as one of the top business priorities world-wide. While in the past, the term BI was used to describe a very broad range of software applications, the latest thinking in this field emphasises IS support for human intelligence, in the context of business decision making. In this unit, students learn how BI helps information discovery and how to analyse multidimensional data. Students gain hands-on experience in using a commercial BI platform. These practical skills, combined with in-depth analytical skills enable students to assist any organization (regardless of its size and industry domain) to derive more intelligence from its data, improve its performance and ultimately, compete on analytics. Issues are explored from the business rather than the technology perspective. This unit does not require prior programming experience.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS3050\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3050/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Business Intelligence for Managers\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period\", \"type\": \"Written exam\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Final exam BringYourOwnDevice exam on campus\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 05\", \"type\": \"Data analysis\", \"length\": \"n/a\", \"weight\": \"3%\", \"description\": \"Tutorial work 1 Tableau tutorial 1 - multiple due dates\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 06\", \"type\": \"Data analysis\", \"length\": \"n/a\", \"weight\": \"7%\", \"description\": \"Tutorial work 2 Tableau tutorial 2 - multiple due dates\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 08 Due date: 26 Apr 2026 at 12:10\", \"type\": \"Written test\", \"length\": \"1 hour\", \"weight\": \"30%\", \"description\": \"Mid-semester exam BringYourOwnDevice exam on campus\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 18 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 28 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Data analysis\", \"length\": \"2500 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Practical Assignment - Data Visualisation Solution and Reflection Analysing the BI process and data visualisation using Tableau\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"-\", \"weight\": \"5%\", \"description\": \"Practical Assignment - Presentation Analysing the BI process and data visualisation using Tableau\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-11T04:52:47.812970+00:00\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction to unit of study\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Foundations for understanding business intelligence\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Data Warehousing: the business perspective\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Data quality and value of information\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Information discovery and decision making\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Designing business intelligence solutions\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Preparation for the mid semester exam and comprehensive case discussion\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Mid-semester exam\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Multi-dimensional modeling, OLAP and ethics\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Business performance management: the role of business intelligence and data visualisation. AI Support for Decision Making.\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Group project work and consultations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Group presentations\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO3 LO4\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Preparation for the final exam\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. use appropriate conceptual frameworks to analyse the role of different business intelligence applications in supporting day to day business and improving organisational and competitive advantage\", \"LO2. describe the organisational issues related to a business intelligence based management approach and propose solutions to overcome the issues\", \"LO3. design a business intelligence solution to address a business problem using a commercial business intelligence platform\", \"LO4. relate business intelligence to business performance management, business process management and information management in an enterprise\", \"LO5. demonstrate an awareness of the current and emerging trends in business intelligence.\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Tutorial work 1 and 2: Students are expected to demonstrate the understanding of the basics of using Tableau and designing a visualisation solution. The tutorials are done during normal class time and must be submitted by the end of the class time.\", \"Mid-semester exam: This exam tests the understanding of the foundational business intelligence concepts.\", \"Practical assignment: Students are required to develop a data visualisation solution to address a business problem using Tableau. A data set will be provided to students. Each group will present their results in the Campus sessions on week 12.\", \"Final exam: The final exam tests the understanding of business intelligence concepts and frameworks in an organisational context. This exam covers all the unit materials. Students will be assessed in a Bring Your Own Device (BYOD) exam taken on campus with supervision. Further information about the delivery of the exams will be provided closer to the test exam.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\"]}'),(38,38,'outline-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:48.828441','Business Process Analysis and Design','{\"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"INFS2020\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"INFS1000 or INFO1000 or INFO1003 or INFO1903\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"Yes\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"This unit provides an in-depth understanding of the role of business processes in effectively and efficiently managing business operations. Students gain essential skills of the entire Business Process Management (BPM) lifecycle, from process identification and design to process monitoring, including process modelling, analysis, redesign, and automation required to achieve high performance and deliver the strategy in a service-oriented environment. Students also gain highly sought after practical skills of using BPM tools to recommend improvements by documenting and simulating current and new processes.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS3120\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3120/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Business Process Analysis and Design\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Formal exam period\", \"type\": \"Written exam\", \"length\": \"2 hours\", \"weight\": \"40%\", \"description\": \"Final Exam Written exam\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 06 Due date: 02 Apr 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 09 Apr 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"Up to 1750 Words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Individual Assignment Written submission\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 09 Due date: 27 Apr 2026 at 09:00 Closing date: 29 Apr 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"Up to 500 words (6 PPT slides)\", \"weight\": \"10%\", \"description\": \"Group Plan Pitch (5 min) Presentation & Submission\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 29 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 30 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Case studies\", \"length\": \"Up to 2750 words\", \"weight\": \"25%\", \"description\": \"Group Project Group Report Written Submission\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-11T04:52:48.827246+00:00\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Week 01\", \"topic\": \"Introduction to the Unit and Business Process Management (BPM)\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 02\", \"topic\": \"Process identification\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 03\", \"topic\": \"Process discovery\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 04\", \"topic\": \"Process modelling (1) - BPMN Notation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 05\", \"topic\": \"Process modelling (2) - As-Is Modelling using BPMN and Signavio\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 06\", \"topic\": \"Process analysis (1) (qualitative)\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 07\", \"topic\": \"Process analysis (2) (quantitative): As-Is Simulation using Signavio\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 08\", \"topic\": \"Process improvement (1) - Redesign heuristics\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO2 LO3 LO4 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 09\", \"topic\": \"Group plan presentations - Feedback collection\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 10\", \"topic\": \"Process improvement (2) - To-Be Simulation using Signavio\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 11\", \"topic\": \"Process implementation, monitoring and mining\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 12\", \"topic\": \"Emerging topics in BPM; Group project consultation\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}, {\"week\": \"Week 13\", \"topic\": \"Unit Review and Final Exam Practice\", \"activity\": \"Seminar (3 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5 LO6\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Discuss the role of Business Process Management (BPM) in a business environment and how BPM initiatives contribute to improving the overall business performance\", \"LO2. Apply the BPM principles, concepts, and frameworks to business problems and practice as captured in the BPM lifecycle\", \"LO3. Address the issues and challenges associated with process improvement initiatives in organisations\", \"LO4. Evaluate the impact of process design and redesign decisions on stakeholders and the role of change management in addressing the risks associated with such changes in organisations\", \"LO5. Use BPM tools to model, document, and analyse processes during the BPM lifecycle stages\", \"LO6. Work independently, collaborate, and communicate in a professional manner with people from diverse backgrounds within a team work environment\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Individual assignment: In this assignment students will take the role of the process analyst and engage in process discovery and analysis within a given business case context.\", \"Group Plan assignment: In this assignment students will analyse the market and identify new services to be executed via new and modified business processes. Students will also develop a project plan to guide the project execution. Students will present and make a written submission.\", \"Group Report assignment: In this assignment students will design new processes following the services proposed in the Group Plan assignment. Students will assess the feasibility of the new processes, the resources required, and the challenges encountered during process implementation. Students will use Signavio (BPM tool) to document, simulate and analyse the new business processes. Students will present a summary of the findings and recommendations.\", \"Final exam: A supervised exam that will test students theoretical understanding, critical thinking, and ability to apply and evaluate BPM concepts and principles in practice.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment will be released as appropiate on Canvas.\"]}'),(39,39,'outline-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:49.381346','Industry and Community Project','{\"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"BUSS3110 or ACCT3400 or BANK3400 or CLAW3400 or FINC3400 or IBUS3400 or MKTG3400 or QBUS3400 or SIEN3400 or WORK3400 or WORK3401\", \"Prerequisites ?\": \"72 credit points\", \"Assumed knowledge ?\": \"INFS1000 and INFS1020 and 6 credit points of INFS 2000-level units of study\", \"Available to study abroad and exchange students\": \"No\"}, \"session\": \"Semester 1, 2026 [Normal day] - Camperdown/Darlington, Sydney\", \"overview\": [\"This unit provides students with the opportunity to address complex problems identified by industry, community, and government organisations. With mentorship from a major industry partner and an academic lead, students integrate their academic skills and knowledge by working in diverse teams across disciplinary boundaries. Students research, analyse and present solutions to a real-world problem, and build on their interpersonal and transferable skills by engaging with and learning from experts in the field. See the ICPU website for further information here: https://www.sydney.edu.au/students/industry-and-community-projects.html.\"], \"unit_code\": \"INFS3400\", \"source_url\": \"https://www.sydney.edu.au/units/INFS3400/2026-S1C-ND-CC\", \"unit_title\": \"Industry and Community Project\", \"assessments\": [{\"due\": \"Ongoing\", \"type\": \"Contribution\", \"length\": \"NA\", \"weight\": \"0%\", \"description\": \"Participation Compulsory 90% class participation\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 03 Due date: 15 Mar 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 25 Mar 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"500 words\", \"weight\": \"0%\", \"description\": \"Individual Statement Instalment 1 Written task\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 05 Due date: 29 Mar 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 08 Apr 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"2000 words\", \"weight\": \"20%\", \"description\": \"Group Plan Research plan\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 07 Due date: 19 Apr 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 29 Apr 2026\", \"type\": \"Portfolio or journal\", \"length\": \"500 words\", \"weight\": \"20%\", \"description\": \"Individual Statement Instalment 2 Written task\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 12 Due date: 18 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 28 May 2026\", \"type\": \"Presentation\", \"length\": \"≤10 minutes\", \"weight\": \"20%\", \"description\": \"Group Presentation Oral presentation inclusive of 300-word or equivalent Executive summary\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 29 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 08 Jun 2026\", \"type\": \"Written work\", \"length\": \"4000 words\", \"weight\": \"40%\", \"description\": \"Group Project Report Report\"}, {\"due\": \"Week 13 Due date: 31 May 2026 at 23:59 Closing date: 10 Jun 2026\", \"type\": \"Evaluation\", \"length\": \"NA\", \"weight\": \"0%\", \"description\": \"Group Work Survey Questionnaire\"}], \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-11T04:52:49.379965+00:00\", \"weekly_schedule\": [{\"week\": \"Weekly\", \"topic\": \"See Canvas site for detailed project schedule\", \"activity\": \"Lecture (1 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}, {\"week\": \"Weekly\", \"topic\": \"See Canvas site for detailed project schedule\", \"activity\": \"Tutorial (2 hr)\", \"learning_outcomes\": \"LO1 LO2 LO3 LO4 LO5\"}], \"learning_outcomes\": [\"LO1. Demonstrate critical awareness of, and apply, disciplinary knowledge, skills and personal attributes to address complex real-world problems\", \"LO2. Assess and integrate diverse stakeholder perspectives to generate innovative insights into complex problems\", \"LO3. Purposefully select and apply complex problem-solving approaches and methods\", \"LO4. Communicate novel ideas effectively and persuasively in professional settings using relevant media\", \"LO5. Demonstrate professionalism (proficiency, adaptivity, and proactivity) in collaborative project work\"], \"assessment_summary\": [\"Group plan aims to articulate the issues the group will address and develop a project plan.\", \"Individual statement aims to analyse and reflect on students’ own and other members’ ways of contributing to their work on this project.\", \"Group report aims to conduct in depth analysis/es and research, discuss the results, and provide recommendations or solutions to the issues outlined in the group plan.\", \"Group presentation including Executive Summary aims to present students’ work and findings to the project partner.\", \"Group work survey aims to evaluate student’s contributions relative to the contributions of other team members.\", \"Attendance aims to ensure active participation by all team members for the project work.\", \"Detailed information for each assessment can be found on Canvas.\", \"Individual contributions to group assessments may adjust the final grade that a student receives, through what is known as a Relative Performance Factor. Please see Canvas Assignment page for more details.\", \"Using AI in ICPU\", \"The Use of Generative AI across multiple sectors of work is increasing exponentially. While use of these tools brings new opportunities such as increased productivity, there are also many potential limitations such as inhibiting the development of analytical and creative thinking skills. Importantly, in ICPU where 80% of assessments are group-based, your use of AI tools must also be negotiated collegially and adhere to the Student Charter 2020, including upholding honesty, ethics, professionalism, and academic integrity (please ensure you are familiar with the updated Academic Integrity 2022 policy).\", \"AI tools permitted\", \"You are permitted to use automated writing tools and generative artificial intelligence (AI) to facilitate your learning and completion of this assignment in individual and group project work. Please adhere to the following guidelines:\", \"Do not enter confidential, personal, copyrighted or otherwise sensitive information into any AI tool, including but not limited to materials shared by industry and/or community partners (noting also the confidentiality clauses that apply in the Student Deed Poll)\", \"If you use these tools, you must be aware of their limitations, biases, and propensity for fabrication\", \"Do not rely on the accuracy of outputs. You remain responsible for your work. This means you must independently verify and edit AI-generated content to ensure the integrity, accuracy, and suitability of the output\", \"While there are many AI tools available, both free and paid, we strongly recommend the use of tools endorsed by the University such as Microsoft Copilot, Cogniti and Adobe Firefly. For a list of AI tools and their uses, see the Different Generative AI Tools page on the AI in Education Canvas site.\", \"Where AI is used, you are advised to keep copies of the prompts you provided and AI outputs as evidence of your research and writing process. This allows you to produce an accurate acknowledgement of AI use when you submit your work, see guidance below. This can be requested by the unit coordinator if there is any uncertainty about the originality of your work.\", \"Acknowledging the use of AI\", \"It is not appropriate to copy-paste AI-generated responses directly as part of your own writing since the work you submit should reflect the understanding you have acquired, your critical engagement with the material and your process of continuous revision and improvement.\", \"Where AI is used, you are advised to keep copies of the prompts you provided and AI outputs as evidence of your research and writing process. This allows you to produce an accurate acknowledgement of AI use when you submit your work, see wording below. This can be requested by the unit coordinator if there is any uncertainty about the originality of your work.\", \"You must acknowledge any uses of AI by inserting a note at the end of your submission where you need to describe the tool(s) that you used, what prompt(s) you provided, how the output of the artificial intelligence was used or adapted by you, and how you verified sources or information if applicable. This additional description does not add to your word count. An example of such an acknowledgement that you may provide might look like:\", \"I acknowledge the use of ChatGPT (https://chat.openai.com/) to refine the academic language of my own work. On date I submitted my entire essay (link to original document here) with the prompt to <“Improve the academic tone and accuracy of language, including grammatical structures, punctuation and vocabulary”>. The output (link here) was then modified further to better represent my own tone and style of writing. One of the limitations I noted was insert limitation and I revised my assessment to address this limitation by insert response.\", \"Please note, your project supervisor may require you to provide a written statement if you would like to use AI heavily in your work, (i.e. for multiple approved uses above or heavy use within any individual use categories), a different approach may be needed including clearly stating the percentage of work attributed to AI for each assessment. If you are unsure or have any concerns, please discuss these with your Project Supervisor in advance. In all cases, individual group members must be transparent with their group members about intended use of generative AI in groupwork.\"]}'),(40,40,'outline-v1','baseline-v1','2026-09-11 04:52:49.884605','Industry and Community Project','{\"details\": {\"Academic unit\": \"Business Information Systems\", \"Credit points\": \"6\", \"Corequisites ?\": \"None\", \"Prohibitions ?\": \"BUSS3110 or ACCT3400 or BANK3400 o